-- finance-cost-and-finance-income-interest-presentation-coverage
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>Interest is the price of time on money, and the same contract produces interest expense for the borrower and interest income for the lender. Both are measured with the effective interest rate on amortised cost. A lender computes interest income on the gross carrying amount until an asset becomes credit-impaired, and from then on on the amount net of the loss allowance.</p></li><li><p>Finance cost is an aggregate. It collects interest on borrowings, interest on lease liabilities, the unwinding of discounted provisions and sometimes net interest on employee benefits, less whatever borrowing cost is capitalised into assets. Each piece comes from a different standard.</p></li><li><p>Where interest lands in profit or loss and in the cash flow statement has been a policy choice under PSAK 201 and PSAK 207. From 1 January 2027 PSAK 118 and its amendments to PSAK 207 remove most of that choice for a non-financial entity: interest on cash and investments goes to the investing category, interest on liabilities to financing, and interest paid and received follow into financing and investing cash flows.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report finance cost of 453,906 for 2024 against 459,285 for 2023, and finance income of 251,561 against 140,407, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of profit or loss and other comprehensive income, Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14; "Daftar" is the printed page). Finance cost is 3.2 percent of revenue in both years. Finance income is 1.8 percent of revenue in 2024, up from 1.0 percent.</p><p>After reading this page a student should be able to accrue interest on either side of a contract, compute interest income on a credit-impaired asset, take a finance cost line apart into its sources, place interest in profit or loss and in cash flows under today's rules and PSAK 118, and compute interest coverage knowing what each version leaves out.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.</p><h2>1. What finance cost and finance income are</h2><p>Interest compensates a lender for parting with money for a period and for the risk of not getting it back. One contract, two mirror entries. A refinery bought for a zero-interest note makes the point (in full dollars): the buyer amortises the 805,103 gap between the note's face value and the refinery's fair value as interest expense, and the party handing over the refinery recognises the same 805,103 as interest income.</p><p>For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary sells surplus equipment with a fair value of 2,000 for a note of 2,805.10 due in five years, with no stated interest, when the market rate is 7 percent.</p><blockquote><p>2,000 × 1.07^5 = 2,805.10, so the note carries 805.10 of interest</p><p>Year 1 interest = 2,000 × 7% = 140.00; year 2 = 2,140 × 7% = 149.80</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 50px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Seller (lender)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Buyer (borrower)</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Notes receivable 140.00 / Cr Finance income 140.00</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Finance cost 140.00 / Cr Notes payable 140.00</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>Finance income</em> in a non-financial entity is mostly interest on bank balances, deposits, loans granted and debt investments, plus dividends where the entity reports them there. <em>Finance cost</em> is broader than "interest on loans". It collects every expense that comes from the passage of time on a liability.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Piece of finance cost</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Source standard</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Owned by</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest on borrowings, including amortised fees (sukuk ijarah: PSAK 410, formerly PSAK 110)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSAK 109 (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Long-term bank loans essay</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest on lease liabilities</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSAK 116 (formerly PSAK 73, IFRS 16)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Right-of-use assets essay</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Unwinding of discounted provisions</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSAK 237 (formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37), ISAK 101</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Decommissioning provision essay</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Net interest on a defined benefit liability, if presented here</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSAK 219 (formerly PSAK 24, IAS 19)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Employee benefits essay</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Less borrowing costs capitalised</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSAK 223 (formerly PSAK 26, IAS 23)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Fixed assets essay</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest on contract liabilities with a significant financing component</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSAK 115 (formerly PSAK 72, IFRS 15)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Deferred revenue essay</p></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>2. How interest accrues: stated rate, effective rate, day count</h2><p>Interest accrues with time, whether or not cash moves. The <em>stated</em> (coupon) rate fixes the cash; the <em>effective interest rate</em> (EIR) is the rate that discounts all contractual cash flows to the initial carrying amount, and it fixes the expense or income. When a bond is priced away from par, or fees are paid, the two differ, and the gap is amortised through interest.</p><p>An interest-bearing note shows both sides of this. A five-year note of 1,000,000 at 8 percent annual interest, issued when the market rate is 10 percent:</p><blockquote><p>PV of principal = 1,000,000 × 0.62092 = 620,920</p><p>PV of interest = 80,000 × 3.79079 = 303,263</p><p>Price = 924,183; discount = 75,817</p><p>Year 1 interest at EIR = 924,183 × 10% = 92,418.30; cash 80,000; discount amortised 12,418.30</p></blockquote><p>The price is below face, so the 75,817 is a discount, and 0.62092 and 3.79079 are the five-year annual factors at 10 percent. Interest expense 92,418.30 exceeds the cash because the discount is being added back to the liability.</p><p><strong>Day count.</strong> Between coupon dates, accrued interest is principal × rate × elapsed time, and the contract decides how time is counted. For example (illustration), a deposit of 10,000 at 5 percent placed on 31 October has accrued 61 days by 31 December.</p><blockquote><p>Actual/365: 10,000 × 5% × 61/365 = 83.56</p><p>Actual/360: 10,000 × 5% × 61/360 = 84.72</p></blockquote><p>If the contract counts actual/360:</p><blockquote><p>Dr Interest receivable 84.72 / Cr Finance income 84.72</p></blockquote><p>The borrower's side of the effective interest method, with fees and floating rates, is taught on the Long-term bank loans page.</p><h2>3. Finance income on financial assets</h2><p>Interest income is recognised only on financial assets that the accounting model treats as earning interest. PSAK 109 sorts debt assets by the <em>business model</em> (hold to collect, hold to collect and sell, or other) and by whether the contractual cash flows are <em>solely payments of principal and interest</em> (SPPI). Assets held to collect with SPPI cash flows are at amortised cost; held to collect and sell, at fair value through other comprehensive income (FVOCI); everything else at fair value through profit or loss (FVTPL). PHE's policy follows this test (Note 3g, Daftar 5/38–5/40, PDF pp. 55–57).</p><table style="min-width: 100px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Amortised cost</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>FVOCI (debt)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>FVTPL</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest income</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>EIR, in profit or loss</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>EIR, in profit or loss</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Part of the fair value change, or shown separately by policy</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Fair value changes</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>not recognised</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>OCI, recycled on sale</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>profit or loss</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Separate "interest revenue (EIR)" line</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>yes</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>yes</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>no</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The last row follows PSAK 201.82(a) (formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1), read with the IFRS Interpretations Committee's March 2018 decision.</p><p><strong>Premium and discount.</strong> For example (illustration), an investor buys a ten-year Garuda bond of 10,000,000 with a 10 percent coupon paid semi-annually when the market rate is 9 percent.</p><blockquote><p>PV of principal = 10,000,000 × 0.41464 = 4,146,400</p><p>PV of interest = 500,000 × 13.00794 = 6,503,970</p><p>Price = 10,650,370; premium = 650,370</p><p>First half-year interest income = 10,650,370 × 4.5% = 479,266.65; premium amortised = 500,000 − 479,266.65 = 20,733.35</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Dr Cash 500,000 / Cr Interest income 479,266.65 / Cr Debt investment 20,733.35</p></blockquote><p><strong>Credit-impaired assets.</strong> Under PSAK 109's expected credit loss (ECL) model, a loss allowance is recognised from day one: twelve-month ECL in stage 1, lifetime ECL in stage 2 after a significant increase in credit risk, and lifetime ECL in stage 3 when the asset is credit-impaired (default, severe financial difficulty, a breach such as missed payments). The allowance changes the interest base only in stage 3. Interest income is the EIR times the <em>gross carrying amount</em>, amortised cost before the allowance, unless the asset is credit-impaired, when it is the EIR times <em>amortised cost</em>, net of the allowance (para. 5.4.1).</p><p>For example (illustration), a subsidiary holds a 10,000 loan to a joint-venture partner at 8 percent, issued at par, so the EIR is 8 percent. Consider the following year under three states at the start of it.</p><table style="min-width: 100px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Stage 1</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Stage 2</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Stage 3</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Loss allowance</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>50</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>900</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest base</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>gross 10,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>gross 10,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>net 6,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest income at 8%</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>800</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>800</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>480</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>In stage 3 the contract still runs at 800 a year. One common way to book it keeps the gross balance contractual and lets the allowance absorb the difference:</p><blockquote><p>Dr Loan, gross 800 / Cr Finance income 480 / Cr Loss allowance 320</p></blockquote><p>If the borrower later pays in full, the unused allowance is reversed as an impairment gain, not as interest income, which is the IFRS Interpretations Committee's 2019 view on a cured asset. The interest in the gross balance never reached profit as interest.</p><p>A write-down recognised only once the loss has occurred, such as an "Alibaba" write-down of 12,680 (illustration), is the old incurred-loss model.</p><p><strong>Purchased credit-impaired assets.</strong> A debt bought already in default earns interest at a <em>credit-adjusted</em> EIR, the rate that discounts the cash flows the buyer expects (after losses) to the price. For example (illustration), a defaulted note bought for 5,500 that is expected to pay 6,050 in a year earns 5,500 × 10% = 550.</p><p><strong>Other sources.</strong> Dividends are recognised when the right to receive them is established. A seller granting long deferred payment terms recognises revenue at the cash price and the financing part as interest income (PSAK 115; see the Revenues essay).</p><h2>4. What goes into finance cost: the pieces owned by other pages</h2><p>Every piece below is the same mechanism, a rate applied to an opening carrying amount, applied to a different liability.</p><p><strong>Borrowings.</strong> A 100,000 three-year loan at 6 percent with a 1,500 fee is carried at 98,500 and charged at its 6.5671 percent EIR, so year-1 finance cost is 6,468.56 against 6,000 of coupon. The fee is spread over the loan's life (see the Long-term bank loans essay).</p><blockquote><p>Dr Finance cost 6,468.56 / Cr Cash 6,000.00 / Cr Bank loans 468.56</p></blockquote><p><strong>Leases.</strong> A lease liability of 17,561.69 at 6 percent accrues interest of 1,053.70 in year 1. Interest is largest in the early years, so a single lease's total charge is front-loaded (see the Right-of-use assets essay).</p><p><strong>Unwinding of provisions.</strong> A decommissioning provision of 68,897 discounted at 5 percent rises by 3,445 in the next year purely because settlement is a year nearer. ISAK 101 sends this to profit or loss as a finance cost and forbids capitalising it (see the Decommissioning provision essay). Unwinding involves no lender and no cash, yet the mathematics is that of interest.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Finance cost 3,445 / Cr Provision for decommissioning 3,445</p></blockquote><p><strong>Net interest on employee benefits.</strong> A net defined benefit liability of 306,640 at a 7.1 percent discount rate gives net interest of 21,771.4. PSAK 219 does not specify where net interest is presented, so some entities show it in finance cost and others, PHE among them, in operating expenses (see the Employee benefits essay).</p><p><strong>Capitalised borrowing costs.</strong> Interest on funds used to build a qualifying asset is part of the asset's cost under PSAK 223. In the fixed-asset illustration, 239.5 of interest goes to construction in progress instead of finance cost (see the Fixed assets essay). Income earned by temporarily investing a specific borrowing reduces the amount capitalised (PSAK 223.12), so it never reaches finance income. Finance cost is therefore interest <em>incurred</em> less interest <em>capitalised</em>. The capitalised part reaches profit later through depreciation, above operating profit.</p><p><strong>Smaller items.</strong> Commitment fees on undrawn facilities, the ineffective part of an interest rate hedge, interest on a contract liability with a significant financing component, and exchange differences on foreign-currency borrowings to the extent they are an adjustment to interest cost (PSAK 223.6(e)) can all appear here.</p><h2>5. Presentation in profit or loss: PSAK 201 now, PSAK 118 from 2027</h2><p>PSAK 201 requires finance costs as a line item (para. 82(b)) and interest revenue by the EIR as another (para. 82(a)), and it forbids offsetting income and expenses unless a standard requires or permits it (para. 32). So finance income and finance cost appear gross, as PHE shows them. "Net finance cost" is an analyst's subtraction.</p><p>What PSAK 201 does not settle is <em>where</em> interest income sits, since it defines no operating profit. Kieso's multiple-step format puts interest a trading company earns on its bank savings in other income inside income from operations, with "financing cost" after it.</p><p>PSAK 118 (IFRS 18), effective 1 January 2027, settles it through categories and two new subtotals, operating profit and profit before financing and income taxes (see the Production expenses essay). For an entity whose main business is neither investing nor lending to customers, interest falls as follows:</p><ul><li><p><em>Investing</em>: income from cash and cash equivalents, from associates and joint ventures accounted for by the equity method, and from investments that generate returns largely independently.</p></li><li><p><em>Financing</em>: all income and expense on liabilities that arise only from raising finance (bank loans, bonds), plus interest expense and the effect of changes in interest rates on other liabilities: leases, provisions, defined benefit liabilities, contract liabilities.</p></li><li><p><em>Operating</em>: everything else, by default.</p></li></ul><p>For example (illustration), an entity has revenue of 10,000, operating expenses of 7,000 (including 50 of net interest on its pension liability), interest income on deposits of 200, a share of profit of an associate of 150, interest on loans of 400 and unwinding of provisions of 100.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Kieso multiple-step</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSAK 118</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Revenue less operating expenses</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>10,000 − 6,950 = 3,050</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest income and associate</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>+ 350, inside income from operations</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>investing category</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Income from operations / operating profit</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,350</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,050</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Profit before financing and income taxes</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>n/a</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,050 + 350 = 3,400</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Finance cost</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(500)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>financing category (550)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Profit before tax</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,850</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,850</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Profit before tax is the same. Operating profit falls from 3,350 to 3,050 because returns on cash and on the associate leave it, and the pension interest moves out of operating expenses into financing.</p><h2>6. Interest in the statement of cash flows</h2><p>Today PSAK 207 <em>Laporan Arus Kas</em> (formerly PSAK 2, IAS 7) gives a non-financial entity a choice, as long as it is applied consistently (para. 31). Interest paid may be operating, because it enters profit, or financing, because it is a cost of obtaining finance. Interest received may be operating or investing (para. 33). Cash flows from interest paid and received must each be disclosed separately (para. 31), and interest paid is reported in total, whether expensed or capitalised (para. 32).</p><p>For example (illustration), an entity collects 3,000 of operating cash before any interest, pays 450 of interest and receives 190 of interest and 60 of dividends from an associate.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Option A: all in operating</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Option B: paid in financing, received in investing</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Operating</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,000 − 450 + 190 + 60 = 2,800</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Investing</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>250</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Financing</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(450)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Net change</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,800</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,800</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The same company reports operating cash flow of 2,800 or 3,000 depending on a policy choice. The amendments to PSAK 207 that come with PSAK 118 end the choice from 1 January 2027 for an entity without a specified main business of investing or lending: interest and dividends paid are financing, interest and dividends received are investing, which is option B. They also make operating profit the starting point of the indirect method.</p><h2>7. Interest coverage</h2><p>Coverage ratios ask how many times earnings could pay the interest bill, which is what lenders and rating agencies watch, and what loan covenants often fix. The basic form is <em>times interest earned</em>:</p><blockquote><p>Interest coverage = earnings before interest and tax (EBIT) / interest expense</p></blockquote><p>Using the figures of section 5 (illustration), with EBIT taken as profit before financing and income taxes, 3,400:</p><blockquote><p>EBIT / all financing expense = 3,400 / 550 = 6.18 times</p><p>EBIT / interest on loans only = 3,400 / 400 = 8.50 times</p><p>EBIT / (550 + 30 of interest capitalised) = 3,400 / 580 = 5.86 times</p><p>(EBIT + 1,200 of depreciation and amortisation) / 550 = 4,600 / 550 = 8.36 times</p></blockquote><p>Leaving unwinding and pension interest out measures the burden of debt that has to be serviced in cash. Adding capitalised interest back measures what the lenders are actually owed, since capitalisation moves interest to the balance sheet without making it cheaper. The EBITDA version, earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation, is closer to cash and is the usual covenant form. <em>Net</em> coverage, which deducts interest income first, flatters a company holding large cash balances, because the cash could be spent.</p><h2>8. Finance cost across industries</h2><p><strong>Banks.</strong> For a bank, interest is revenue. Interest income on loans and interest expense on deposits are the top of the income statement, and the key measure is <em>net interest income</em> over average earning assets, the net interest margin (NIM). For example (illustration), interest income 9,000, interest expense 4,000, average earning assets 100,000.</p><blockquote><p>Net interest income = 9,000 − 4,000 = 5,000; NIM = 5,000 / 100,000 = 5.0%</p></blockquote><p>Banks usually classify interest paid and received as operating cash flows (PSAK 207.33). PSAK 118 keeps that through its exception for entities that lend to customers as a main business, which present such interest in the operating category.</p><p><strong>Islamic finance.</strong> A sharia entity earns no interest. Under <em>murabahah</em> (PSAK 402, formerly PSAK 102) the bank buys an asset and sells it at cost plus an agreed margin payable in instalments, and the margin is recognised over the financing period. Under <em>mudharabah</em> (PSAK 405, formerly PSAK 105) funds are shared in profit by an agreed ratio (<em>nisbah</em>). For example (illustration), income of 500 earned on depositors' mudharabah funds with a 40 percent depositor nisbah gives them 200 of <em>bagi hasil</em>. The holders' share is presented as a distribution to <em>temporary syirkah funds</em>, not as finance cost. A non-sharia company that borrows through a sharia instrument, such as the sukuk ijarah on the Long-term bank loans page, shows the ijarah fee as an expense in its finance line.</p><p><strong>Real estate developers.</strong> Interest on loans for projects under construction is capitalised into inventory property and reaches profit through cost of sales when units are sold. PSAK 223 lets an entity skip capitalisation for inventories produced in large quantities on a repetitive basis, but developers commonly capitalise. Reported finance cost then understates the debt burden. For example (illustration), a developer incurs 400 of interest, capitalises 300 and expenses 100, sells 60 percent of the units so 180 of capitalised interest passes through cost of sales, and reports EBIT of 2,000.</p><blockquote><p>Reported coverage = 2,000 / 100 = 20.0 times</p><p>Adjusted = (2,000 + 180) / 400 = 5.45 times</p></blockquote><h2>9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Notes 40 and 41</h2><p>PSAK 107 (formerly PSAK 60, IFRS 7) asks for total interest income and expense on financial instruments not at FVTPL. PHE gives both notes on one page (Note 40 and Note 41, Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167).</p><table style="min-width: 100px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Finance cost</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>%</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest expense from loans</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>297,328</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>65.5</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>311,684</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Accretion expense (unwinding of decommissioning provision)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>119,925</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>26.4</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>113,221</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest expense from leases</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>17,225</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3.8</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>19,987</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Other interest expenses</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>19,428</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4.3</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>14,393</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>453,906</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>459,285</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Finance income</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest income from cash in banks and time deposits</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>251,561</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>140,234</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Adjustment of hedging portion</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>173</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>251,561</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>140,407</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Both totals agree with the face of the statement. Finance cost is almost flat while its mix shifted. Loan interest fell by 14,356 as borrowings were repaid (see the Long-term bank loans essay), and accretion rose by 6,704. More than a quarter of the line is unwinding, a non-cash charge.</p><p>Several other pieces sit outside the line. Borrowing costs of 2,277 were capitalised into the Jambaran-Tiung Biru project (Note 12, Daftar 5/109, PDF p. 126; 11,321 in 2023), so interest incurred was at least 456,183. Net interest on employee benefits, interest cost of 46,048 less interest income on plan assets of 20,760, is 25,288, charged in cost of revenues and general and administrative expenses (Note 25b, Daftar 5/123, PDF p. 140). A share in the net profit of associates and joint ventures of 162,620 is inside other income, net (Note 42, PDF p. 167).</p><blockquote><p>Net finance cost = 453,906 − 251,561 = 202,345 (2023: 318,878)</p><p>Implied yield = 251,561 / average cash and cash equivalents 3,816,529 = 6.6%; on cash plus restricted cash, 251,561 / 5,755,990 = 4.4% (2023: 2.96% on cash)</p></blockquote><p>US dollar time deposits paid 0.75–6.92 percent in 2024 against 0.22–1.10 percent in 2023 (Note 6, Daftar 5/93, PDF p. 110), which fits the jump in interest income while cash fell from 5,018,739 to 2,614,318. How much of the restricted cash earns interest for PHE is not stated, so the true yield lies between the two figures.</p><blockquote><p>Interest coverage = (4,892,576 + 453,906) / 453,906 = 5,346,482 / 453,906 = 11.78 times (2023: 10.78)</p><p>Excluding accretion = 5,346,482 / 333,981 = 16.01 times</p><p>(EBIT + DD&amp;A 2,443,000 + impairment 215,666, added back as covenants usually do) / 453,906 = 8,005,148 / 453,906 = 17.64 times</p></blockquote><p>The covenant on PHE's corporate facilities requires EBITDA of at least 3.00 times interest (Note 27, Daftar 5/136 and 5/140, PDF pp. 153 and 157). Its exact definitions are not disclosed, but on every version above the headroom is wide.</p><p>Recast under PSAK 118, the 251,561 of interest income and the 162,620 share of associates leave operating profit for investing, and all of Note 41 sits in financing; the Production expenses essay works the numbers.</p><p>The cash flow statement, prepared on the direct method, has no line for interest paid or received (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17). PSAK 207.31 asks for both. Interest paid may sit in "cash paid from other financing activities" (328,119), and interest received in operating receipts, but PHE does not say.</p><h2>10. Upstream note</h2><p>Accretion is large because decommissioning provisions are large. An upstream group carries the discounted cost of plugging wells and removing platforms for decades, so its finance cost includes a slice that no lender earns. PHE's interest-rate swaps on the Jambaran-Tiung Biru loans also touch the line: the ineffective part of the hedge, 1,395 in 2024, is "included in finance costs" (Note 14, Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123), presumably within other interest expenses. Whether interest is recoverable under PHE's PSCs is not disclosed in the FS.</p><h2>11. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><code>finance-income</code>: covered on this page.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/long-term-bank-loans-non-current">long-term-bank-loans-non-current</a>, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/long-term-bank-loans-non-current">short-term-bank-loans</a>, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/long-term-bank-loans-non-current">sukuk-payables</a>: interest at the effective rate.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/right-of-use-assets-net">lease-liabilities-current</a>, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/right-of-use-assets-net">lease-liabilities-non-current</a>, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/due-from-government">due-to-government-non-current</a>: lease interest, including state-owned-asset finance leases.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/decommissioning-site-restoration-provision">decommissioning-site-restoration-provision</a>: the accretion.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/employee-benefits-liabilities-non-current">employee-benefits-liabilities-non-current</a>: net interest, presented in operating expenses by PHE.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net">fixed-assets-net</a>, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net">oil-gas-properties-net</a>: capitalised borrowing costs.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/cash-and-cash-equivalents">cash-and-cash-equivalents</a>, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/restricted-cash">restricted-cash-non-current</a>: the source of interest income.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/production-expenses">other-income-expense-net</a>: holds the share of associates' profit, an investing item under PSAK 118.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/foreign-exchange-loss-net">foreign-exchange-loss-net</a>: exchange differences on borrowings.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>That PHE's cash flow statement lacks the separate interest disclosure PSAK 207.31 requires is read from the face of the statement and Note 50; the amounts may be disclosed somewhere not found. Where interest paid sits is a guess. Similarly, showing the share of associates' profit inside other income, net looks short of PSAK 201.82(c), which asks for it on the face.</p></li><li><p>Note 41 lease interest of 17,225 exceeds the 7,354 in the Note 30 movement (Daftar 5/146, PDF p. 163) by 9,871. The difference is consistent with interest on the state-owned-asset finance leases carried in Note 20 (63,825; unamortised interest down from 88,583 to 74,046, Daftar 5/114–5/115, PDF pp. 131–132) and on third-party LPG plant and pipeline leases, both named in Note 41's narrative. PHE gives no split (see the Right-of-use assets essay). Segment eliminations of intra-group interest differ between finance income (27,836) and finance cost (28,975) by 1,139, unexplained. Interest income on plan assets is 22,333 in the plan-asset movement but 20,760 in the profit-or-loss table (Note 25b).</p></li><li><p>The 4.4–6.6 percent implied yield assumes interest income relates to average cash balances, with or without restricted cash. Restricted cash is mostly site-restoration funds plus guarantee collateral and required deposits (Note 7, Daftar 5/95, PDF p. 112), and restoration funds moved by SKK Migas carry their interest with them (Note 14b, PDF p. 123), so not all of it earns interest for PHE. PHE does not say which balances earn the 251,561.</p></li><li><p>The murabahah and mudharabah descriptions and PSAK 223's exemption for repetitive inventories are from knowledge, not checked against the standards' text.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of profit or loss and OCI (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 2 basis of preparation, direct method (Daftar 5/29, PDF p. 46); Note 3g financial instruments, SPPI, EIR (Daftar 5/38–5/40 and 5/46–5/47, PDF pp. 55–57 and 63–64); Note 3aa borrowing costs (Daftar 5/76, PDF p. 93); Note 6 (Daftar 5/93, PDF p. 110); Note 7 (Daftar 5/95, PDF p. 112); Note 12 (Daftar 5/109, PDF p. 126); Note 14 hedge ineffectiveness (Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123); Note 20 (Daftar 5/114–5/115, PDF pp. 131–132); Note 25b (Daftar 5/122–5/123, PDF pp. 139–140); Note 27 (Daftar 5/136 and 5/140, PDF pp. 153 and 157); Note 30 (Daftar 5/146, PDF p. 163); Notes 40, 41 and 42 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167); Note 46 segment information (Daftar 5/220, PDF p. 237); Note 50 (Daftar 5/235, PDF p. 252). Totals checked against the PHE FS 2024 data extract. VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 109 (IFRS 9), para. 5.4.1 (gross carrying amount; amortised cost for credit-impaired assets) and Appendix A definition of gross carrying amount: VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation agenda decision "Curing of a credit-impaired financial asset", March 2019; IAS Plus meeting notes). Credit-adjusted EIR for purchased credit-impaired assets, dividends: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 201 (IAS 1), para. 82(a): VERIFIED (web, IFRS Interpretations Committee agenda decision "Presentation of interest revenue for particular financial instruments", March 2018). Paras. 82(b) and 82(c): VERIFIED (web, IFRS Interpretations Committee July 2013 agenda paper 12, reproducing IAS 1.82). Para. 32: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 118 <em>Penyajian dan Pengungkapan dalam Laporan Keuangan</em> (IFRS 18): ratified by DSAK IAI 28 May 2025, effective 1 January 2027, replaces PSAK 201, required subtotals: VERIFIED (web, IAI <em>DSAK Terkini</em> vol. 7 2025; Binus Accounting, Aug 2026; Fortune IDN). Categories and contents of investing and financing for entities without a specified main business: VERIFIED (web, ACCA DipIFR technical article "IFRS 18"; BDO). Consequential amendments to IAS 7 (interest and dividends paid → financing, received → investing; operating profit as indirect-method starting point; effective 1 January 2027): VERIFIED (web, KPMG "IFRS 18 changes to the statement of cash flows", 2026; EY Applying IFRS 18, 2025–2026).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 207 (IAS 7), paras. 31–33: VERIFIED (web, IFRS staff paper "Classification of interest paid that is capitalised", May 2011, quoting the paragraphs; IAS Plus).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 219 (IAS 19): no prescribed presentation of service cost and net interest: VERIFIED (web, secondary: IAI "IAS 19R Employee Benefits – Indonesia" training slides).</p></li><li><p>PSAK numbers 109 (71), 201 (1), 207 (2), 115 (72), 116 (73), 219 (24), 223 (26), 237 (57), 107 (60), ISAK 101 (ISAK 9): from IAI's renumbering rule, as in the sibling essays. PSAK 410 (110 Akuntansi Sukuk), PSAK 402 (102 Murabahah) and PSAK 405 (105 Mudharabah): VERIFIED (web, IAI "Komparasi Perubahan Penomoran PSAK ISAK SAK Indonesia"). Content of PSAK 223.6(e), 223.12 and the repetitive-inventory exemption, PSAK 402, PSAK 405, PSAK 107: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting</em>, IFRS edition, chs. 4, 14 and 17. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest is the price of time on money, and the same contract produces interest expense for the borrower and interest income for the lender. Both are measured with the effective interest rate on amortised cost. A lender computes interest income on the gross carrying amount until an asset becomes credit-impaired, and from then on on the amount net of the loss allowance.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Finance cost is an aggregate. It collects interest on borrowings, interest on lease liabilities, the unwinding of discounted provisions and sometimes net interest on employee benefits, less whatever borrowing cost is capitalised into assets. Each piece comes from a different standard.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Where interest lands in profit or loss and in the cash flow statement has been a policy choice under PSAK 201 and PSAK 207. From 1 January 2027 PSAK 118 and its amendments to PSAK 207 remove most of that choice for a non-financial entity: interest on cash and investments goes to the investing category, interest on liabilities to financing, and interest paid and received follow into financing and investing cash flows.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report finance cost of 453,906 for 2024 against 459,285 for 2023, and finance income of 251,561 against 140,407, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of profit or loss and other comprehensive income, Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). Finance cost is 3.2 percent of revenue in both years. Finance income is 1.8 percent of revenue in 2024, up from 1.0 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After reading this page a student should be able to accrue interest on either side of a contract, compute interest income on a credit-impaired asset, take a finance cost line apart into its sources, place interest in profit or loss and in cash flows under today's rules and PSAK 118, and compute interest coverage knowing what each version leaves out.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. What finance cost and finance income are","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest compensates a lender for parting with money for a period and for the risk of not getting it back. One contract, two mirror entries. A refinery bought for a zero-interest note makes the point (in full dollars): the buyer amortises the 805,103 gap between the note's face value and the refinery's fair value as interest expense, and the party handing over the refinery recognises the same 805,103 as interest income.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary sells surplus equipment with a fair value of 2,000 for a note of 2,805.10 due in five years, with no stated interest, when the market rate is 7 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,000 × 1.07^5 = 2,805.10, so the note carries 805.10 of interest","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 1 interest = 2,000 × 7% = 140.00; year 2 = 2,140 × 7% = 149.80","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Seller (lender)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Buyer (borrower)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Notes receivable 140.00 / Cr Finance income 140.00","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Finance cost 140.00 / Cr Notes payable 140.00","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Finance income","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" in a non-financial entity is mostly interest on bank balances, deposits, loans granted and debt investments, plus dividends where the entity reports them there. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Finance cost","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is broader than \"interest on loans\". It collects every expense that comes from the passage of time on a liability.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Piece of finance cost","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Source standard","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Owned by","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest on borrowings, including amortised fees (sukuk ijarah: PSAK 410, formerly PSAK 110)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 109 (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Long-term bank loans essay","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest on lease liabilities","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 116 (formerly PSAK 73, IFRS 16)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Right-of-use assets essay","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Unwinding of discounted provisions","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 237 (formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37), ISAK 101","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Decommissioning provision essay","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net interest on a defined benefit liability, if presented here","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 219 (formerly PSAK 24, IAS 19)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Employee benefits essay","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Less borrowing costs capitalised","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 223 (formerly PSAK 26, IAS 23)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fixed assets essay","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest on contract liabilities with a significant financing component","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 115 (formerly PSAK 72, IFRS 15)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Deferred revenue essay","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. How interest accrues: stated rate, effective rate, day count","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest accrues with time, whether or not cash moves. The ","type":"text"},{"text":"stated","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (coupon) rate fixes the cash; the ","type":"text"},{"text":"effective interest rate","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (EIR) is the rate that discounts all contractual cash flows to the initial carrying amount, and it fixes the expense or income. When a bond is priced away from par, or fees are paid, the two differ, and the gap is amortised through interest.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An interest-bearing note shows both sides of this. A five-year note of 1,000,000 at 8 percent annual interest, issued when the market rate is 10 percent:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PV of principal = 1,000,000 × 0.62092 = 620,920","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PV of interest = 80,000 × 3.79079 = 303,263","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Price = 924,183; discount = 75,817","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 1 interest at EIR = 924,183 × 10% = 92,418.30; cash 80,000; discount amortised 12,418.30","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The price is below face, so the 75,817 is a discount, and 0.62092 and 3.79079 are the five-year annual factors at 10 percent. Interest expense 92,418.30 exceeds the cash because the discount is being added back to the liability.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Day count.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Between coupon dates, accrued interest is principal × rate × elapsed time, and the contract decides how time is counted. For example (illustration), a deposit of 10,000 at 5 percent placed on 31 October has accrued 61 days by 31 December.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Actual/365: 10,000 × 5% × 61/365 = 83.56","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Actual/360: 10,000 × 5% × 61/360 = 84.72","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"If the contract counts actual/360:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Interest receivable 84.72 / Cr Finance income 84.72","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The borrower's side of the effective interest method, with fees and floating rates, is taught on the Long-term bank loans page.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Finance income on financial assets","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest income is recognised only on financial assets that the accounting model treats as earning interest. PSAK 109 sorts debt assets by the ","type":"text"},{"text":"business model","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (hold to collect, hold to collect and sell, or other) and by whether the contractual cash flows are ","type":"text"},{"text":"solely payments of principal and interest","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (SPPI). Assets held to collect with SPPI cash flows are at amortised cost; held to collect and sell, at fair value through other comprehensive income (FVOCI); everything else at fair value through profit or loss (FVTPL). PHE's policy follows this test (Note 3g, Daftar 5/38–5/40, PDF pp. 55–57).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Amortised cost","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"FVOCI (debt)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"FVTPL","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest income","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"EIR, in profit or loss","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"EIR, in profit or loss","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Part of the fair value change, or shown separately by policy","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fair value changes","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"not recognised","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"OCI, recycled on sale","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"profit or loss","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Separate \"interest revenue (EIR)\" line","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"yes","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"yes","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The last row follows PSAK 201.82(a) (formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1), read with the IFRS Interpretations Committee's March 2018 decision.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Premium and discount.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" For example (illustration), an investor buys a ten-year Garuda bond of 10,000,000 with a 10 percent coupon paid semi-annually when the market rate is 9 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PV of principal = 10,000,000 × 0.41464 = 4,146,400","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PV of interest = 500,000 × 13.00794 = 6,503,970","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Price = 10,650,370; premium = 650,370","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"First half-year interest income = 10,650,370 × 4.5% = 479,266.65; premium amortised = 500,000 − 479,266.65 = 20,733.35","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 500,000 / Cr Interest income 479,266.65 / Cr Debt investment 20,733.35","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Credit-impaired assets.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Under PSAK 109's expected credit loss (ECL) model, a loss allowance is recognised from day one: twelve-month ECL in stage 1, lifetime ECL in stage 2 after a significant increase in credit risk, and lifetime ECL in stage 3 when the asset is credit-impaired (default, severe financial difficulty, a breach such as missed payments). The allowance changes the interest base only in stage 3. Interest income is the EIR times the ","type":"text"},{"text":"gross carrying amount","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", amortised cost before the allowance, unless the asset is credit-impaired, when it is the EIR times ","type":"text"},{"text":"amortised cost","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", net of the allowance (para. 5.4.1).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a subsidiary holds a 10,000 loan to a joint-venture partner at 8 percent, issued at par, so the EIR is 8 percent. Consider the following year under three states at the start of it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Stage 1","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Stage 2","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Stage 3","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Loss allowance","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"50","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"900","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest base","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"gross 10,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"gross 10,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"net 6,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest income at 8%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"480","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In stage 3 the contract still runs at 800 a year. One common way to book it keeps the gross balance contractual and lets the allowance absorb the difference:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Loan, gross 800 / Cr Finance income 480 / Cr Loss allowance 320","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"If the borrower later pays in full, the unused allowance is reversed as an impairment gain, not as interest income, which is the IFRS Interpretations Committee's 2019 view on a cured asset. The interest in the gross balance never reached profit as interest.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A write-down recognised only once the loss has occurred, such as an \"Alibaba\" write-down of 12,680 (illustration), is the old incurred-loss model.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Purchased credit-impaired assets.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A debt bought already in default earns interest at a ","type":"text"},{"text":"credit-adjusted","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" EIR, the rate that discounts the cash flows the buyer expects (after losses) to the price. For example (illustration), a defaulted note bought for 5,500 that is expected to pay 6,050 in a year earns 5,500 × 10% = 550.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other sources.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Dividends are recognised when the right to receive them is established. A seller granting long deferred payment terms recognises revenue at the cash price and the financing part as interest income (PSAK 115; see the Revenues essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. What goes into finance cost: the pieces owned by other pages","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Every piece below is the same mechanism, a rate applied to an opening carrying amount, applied to a different liability.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Borrowings.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A 100,000 three-year loan at 6 percent with a 1,500 fee is carried at 98,500 and charged at its 6.5671 percent EIR, so year-1 finance cost is 6,468.56 against 6,000 of coupon. The fee is spread over the loan's life (see the Long-term bank loans essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Finance cost 6,468.56 / Cr Cash 6,000.00 / Cr Bank loans 468.56","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Leases.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A lease liability of 17,561.69 at 6 percent accrues interest of 1,053.70 in year 1. Interest is largest in the early years, so a single lease's total charge is front-loaded (see the Right-of-use assets essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Unwinding of provisions.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A decommissioning provision of 68,897 discounted at 5 percent rises by 3,445 in the next year purely because settlement is a year nearer. ISAK 101 sends this to profit or loss as a finance cost and forbids capitalising it (see the Decommissioning provision essay). Unwinding involves no lender and no cash, yet the mathematics is that of interest.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Finance cost 3,445 / Cr Provision for decommissioning 3,445","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net interest on employee benefits.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A net defined benefit liability of 306,640 at a 7.1 percent discount rate gives net interest of 21,771.4. PSAK 219 does not specify where net interest is presented, so some entities show it in finance cost and others, PHE among them, in operating expenses (see the Employee benefits essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Capitalised borrowing costs.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Interest on funds used to build a qualifying asset is part of the asset's cost under PSAK 223. In the fixed-asset illustration, 239.5 of interest goes to construction in progress instead of finance cost (see the Fixed assets essay). Income earned by temporarily investing a specific borrowing reduces the amount capitalised (PSAK 223.12), so it never reaches finance income. Finance cost is therefore interest ","type":"text"},{"text":"incurred","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" less interest ","type":"text"},{"text":"capitalised","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". The capitalised part reaches profit later through depreciation, above operating profit.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Smaller items.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Commitment fees on undrawn facilities, the ineffective part of an interest rate hedge, interest on a contract liability with a significant financing component, and exchange differences on foreign-currency borrowings to the extent they are an adjustment to interest cost (PSAK 223.6(e)) can all appear here.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Presentation in profit or loss: PSAK 201 now, PSAK 118 from 2027","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 201 requires finance costs as a line item (para. 82(b)) and interest revenue by the EIR as another (para. 82(a)), and it forbids offsetting income and expenses unless a standard requires or permits it (para. 32). So finance income and finance cost appear gross, as PHE shows them. \"Net finance cost\" is an analyst's subtraction.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"What PSAK 201 does not settle is ","type":"text"},{"text":"where","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" interest income sits, since it defines no operating profit. Kieso's multiple-step format puts interest a trading company earns on its bank savings in other income inside income from operations, with \"financing cost\" after it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 118 (IFRS 18), effective 1 January 2027, settles it through categories and two new subtotals, operating profit and profit before financing and income taxes (see the Production expenses essay). For an entity whose main business is neither investing nor lending to customers, interest falls as follows:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Investing","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": income from cash and cash equivalents, from associates and joint ventures accounted for by the equity method, and from investments that generate returns largely independently.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Financing","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": all income and expense on liabilities that arise only from raising finance (bank loans, bonds), plus interest expense and the effect of changes in interest rates on other liabilities: leases, provisions, defined benefit liabilities, contract liabilities.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Operating","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": everything else, by default.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), an entity has revenue of 10,000, operating expenses of 7,000 (including 50 of net interest on its pension liability), interest income on deposits of 200, a share of profit of an associate of 150, interest on loans of 400 and unwinding of provisions of 100.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso multiple-step","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 118","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Revenue less operating expenses","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10,000 − 6,950 = 3,050","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest income and associate","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"+ 350, inside income from operations","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"investing category","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Income from operations / operating profit","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,350","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,050","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Profit before financing and income taxes","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"n/a","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,050 + 350 = 3,400","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Finance cost","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(500)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"financing category (550)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Profit before tax","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,850","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,850","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Profit before tax is the same. Operating profit falls from 3,350 to 3,050 because returns on cash and on the associate leave it, and the pension interest moves out of operating expenses into financing.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Interest in the statement of cash flows","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Today PSAK 207 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Laporan Arus Kas","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 2, IAS 7) gives a non-financial entity a choice, as long as it is applied consistently (para. 31). Interest paid may be operating, because it enters profit, or financing, because it is a cost of obtaining finance. Interest received may be operating or investing (para. 33). Cash flows from interest paid and received must each be disclosed separately (para. 31), and interest paid is reported in total, whether expensed or capitalised (para. 32).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), an entity collects 3,000 of operating cash before any interest, pays 450 of interest and receives 190 of interest and 60 of dividends from an associate.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Option A: all in operating","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Option B: paid in financing, received in investing","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Operating","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,000 − 450 + 190 + 60 = 2,800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Investing","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"250","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Financing","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(450)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net change","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,800","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The same company reports operating cash flow of 2,800 or 3,000 depending on a policy choice. The amendments to PSAK 207 that come with PSAK 118 end the choice from 1 January 2027 for an entity without a specified main business of investing or lending: interest and dividends paid are financing, interest and dividends received are investing, which is option B. They also make operating profit the starting point of the indirect method.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Interest coverage","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Coverage ratios ask how many times earnings could pay the interest bill, which is what lenders and rating agencies watch, and what loan covenants often fix. The basic form is ","type":"text"},{"text":"times interest earned","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":":","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest coverage = earnings before interest and tax (EBIT) / interest expense","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Using the figures of section 5 (illustration), with EBIT taken as profit before financing and income taxes, 3,400:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"EBIT / all financing expense = 3,400 / 550 = 6.18 times","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"EBIT / interest on loans only = 3,400 / 400 = 8.50 times","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"EBIT / (550 + 30 of interest capitalised) = 3,400 / 580 = 5.86 times","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(EBIT + 1,200 of depreciation and amortisation) / 550 = 4,600 / 550 = 8.36 times","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Leaving unwinding and pension interest out measures the burden of debt that has to be serviced in cash. Adding capitalised interest back measures what the lenders are actually owed, since capitalisation moves interest to the balance sheet without making it cheaper. The EBITDA version, earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation, is closer to cash and is the usual covenant form. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" coverage, which deducts interest income first, flatters a company holding large cash balances, because the cash could be spent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Finance cost across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Banks.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" For a bank, interest is revenue. Interest income on loans and interest expense on deposits are the top of the income statement, and the key measure is ","type":"text"},{"text":"net interest income","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" over average earning assets, the net interest margin (NIM). For example (illustration), interest income 9,000, interest expense 4,000, average earning assets 100,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net interest income = 9,000 − 4,000 = 5,000; NIM = 5,000 / 100,000 = 5.0%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Banks usually classify interest paid and received as operating cash flows (PSAK 207.33). PSAK 118 keeps that through its exception for entities that lend to customers as a main business, which present such interest in the operating category.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Islamic finance.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A sharia entity earns no interest. Under ","type":"text"},{"text":"murabahah","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (PSAK 402, formerly PSAK 102) the bank buys an asset and sells it at cost plus an agreed margin payable in instalments, and the margin is recognised over the financing period. Under ","type":"text"},{"text":"mudharabah","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (PSAK 405, formerly PSAK 105) funds are shared in profit by an agreed ratio (","type":"text"},{"text":"nisbah","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"). For example (illustration), income of 500 earned on depositors' mudharabah funds with a 40 percent depositor nisbah gives them 200 of ","type":"text"},{"text":"bagi hasil","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". The holders' share is presented as a distribution to ","type":"text"},{"text":"temporary syirkah funds","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", not as finance cost. A non-sharia company that borrows through a sharia instrument, such as the sukuk ijarah on the Long-term bank loans page, shows the ijarah fee as an expense in its finance line.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Real estate developers.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Interest on loans for projects under construction is capitalised into inventory property and reaches profit through cost of sales when units are sold. PSAK 223 lets an entity skip capitalisation for inventories produced in large quantities on a repetitive basis, but developers commonly capitalise. Reported finance cost then understates the debt burden. For example (illustration), a developer incurs 400 of interest, capitalises 300 and expenses 100, sells 60 percent of the units so 180 of capitalised interest passes through cost of sales, and reports EBIT of 2,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Reported coverage = 2,000 / 100 = 20.0 times","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Adjusted = (2,000 + 180) / 400 = 5.45 times","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Notes 40 and 41","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 107 (formerly PSAK 60, IFRS 7) asks for total interest income and expense on financial instruments not at FVTPL. PHE gives both notes on one page (Note 40 and Note 41, Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Finance cost","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2023","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest expense from loans","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"297,328","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"65.5","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"311,684","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accretion expense (unwinding of decommissioning provision)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"119,925","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"26.4","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"113,221","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest expense from leases","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"17,225","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3.8","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"19,987","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other interest expenses","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"19,428","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4.3","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"14,393","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"453,906","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"459,285","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Finance income","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2023","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest income from cash in banks and time deposits","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"251,561","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"140,234","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Adjustment of hedging portion","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"173","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"251,561","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"140,407","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Both totals agree with the face of the statement. Finance cost is almost flat while its mix shifted. Loan interest fell by 14,356 as borrowings were repaid (see the Long-term bank loans essay), and accretion rose by 6,704. More than a quarter of the line is unwinding, a non-cash charge.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Several other pieces sit outside the line. Borrowing costs of 2,277 were capitalised into the Jambaran-Tiung Biru project (Note 12, Daftar 5/109, PDF p. 126; 11,321 in 2023), so interest incurred was at least 456,183. Net interest on employee benefits, interest cost of 46,048 less interest income on plan assets of 20,760, is 25,288, charged in cost of revenues and general and administrative expenses (Note 25b, Daftar 5/123, PDF p. 140). A share in the net profit of associates and joint ventures of 162,620 is inside other income, net (Note 42, PDF p. 167).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net finance cost = 453,906 − 251,561 = 202,345 (2023: 318,878)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Implied yield = 251,561 / average cash and cash equivalents 3,816,529 = 6.6%; on cash plus restricted cash, 251,561 / 5,755,990 = 4.4% (2023: 2.96% on cash)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"US dollar time deposits paid 0.75–6.92 percent in 2024 against 0.22–1.10 percent in 2023 (Note 6, Daftar 5/93, PDF p. 110), which fits the jump in interest income while cash fell from 5,018,739 to 2,614,318. How much of the restricted cash earns interest for PHE is not stated, so the true yield lies between the two figures.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest coverage = (4,892,576 + 453,906) / 453,906 = 5,346,482 / 453,906 = 11.78 times (2023: 10.78)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Excluding accretion = 5,346,482 / 333,981 = 16.01 times","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(EBIT + DD&A 2,443,000 + impairment 215,666, added back as covenants usually do) / 453,906 = 8,005,148 / 453,906 = 17.64 times","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The covenant on PHE's corporate facilities requires EBITDA of at least 3.00 times interest (Note 27, Daftar 5/136 and 5/140, PDF pp. 153 and 157). Its exact definitions are not disclosed, but on every version above the headroom is wide.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Recast under PSAK 118, the 251,561 of interest income and the 162,620 share of associates leave operating profit for investing, and all of Note 41 sits in financing; the Production expenses essay works the numbers.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The cash flow statement, prepared on the direct method, has no line for interest paid or received (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17). PSAK 207.31 asks for both. Interest paid may sit in \"cash paid from other financing activities\" (328,119), and interest received in operating receipts, but PHE does not say.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Upstream note","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accretion is large because decommissioning provisions are large. An upstream group carries the discounted cost of plugging wells and removing platforms for decades, so its finance cost includes a slice that no lender earns. PHE's interest-rate swaps on the Jambaran-Tiung Biru loans also touch the line: the ineffective part of the hedge, 1,395 in 2024, is \"included in finance costs\" (Note 14, Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123), presumably within other interest expenses. Whether interest is recoverable under PHE's PSCs is not disclosed in the FS.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"finance-income","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":": covered on this page.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"long-term-bank-loans-non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/long-term-bank-loans-non-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"text":"short-term-bank-loans","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/long-term-bank-loans-non-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"text":"sukuk-payables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/long-term-bank-loans-non-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": interest at the effective rate.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"lease-liabilities-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/right-of-use-assets-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"text":"lease-liabilities-non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/right-of-use-assets-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"text":"due-to-government-non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/due-from-government","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": lease interest, including state-owned-asset finance leases.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"decommissioning-site-restoration-provision","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/decommissioning-site-restoration-provision","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the accretion.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"employee-benefits-liabilities-non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/employee-benefits-liabilities-non-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": net interest, presented in operating expenses by PHE.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"fixed-assets-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"text":"oil-gas-properties-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": capitalised borrowing costs.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"cash-and-cash-equivalents","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/cash-and-cash-equivalents","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"text":"restricted-cash-non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/restricted-cash","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the source of interest income.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other-income-expense-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/production-expenses","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": holds the share of associates' profit, an investing item under PSAK 118.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"foreign-exchange-loss-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/foreign-exchange-loss-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": exchange differences on borrowings.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That PHE's cash flow statement lacks the separate interest disclosure PSAK 207.31 requires is read from the face of the statement and Note 50; the amounts may be disclosed somewhere not found. Where interest paid sits is a guess. Similarly, showing the share of associates' profit inside other income, net looks short of PSAK 201.82(c), which asks for it on the face.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 41 lease interest of 17,225 exceeds the 7,354 in the Note 30 movement (Daftar 5/146, PDF p. 163) by 9,871. The difference is consistent with interest on the state-owned-asset finance leases carried in Note 20 (63,825; unamortised interest down from 88,583 to 74,046, Daftar 5/114–5/115, PDF pp. 131–132) and on third-party LPG plant and pipeline leases, both named in Note 41's narrative. PHE gives no split (see the Right-of-use assets essay). Segment eliminations of intra-group interest differ between finance income (27,836) and finance cost (28,975) by 1,139, unexplained. Interest income on plan assets is 22,333 in the plan-asset movement but 20,760 in the profit-or-loss table (Note 25b).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 4.4–6.6 percent implied yield assumes interest income relates to average cash balances, with or without restricted cash. Restricted cash is mostly site-restoration funds plus guarantee collateral and required deposits (Note 7, Daftar 5/95, PDF p. 112), and restoration funds moved by SKK Migas carry their interest with them (Note 14b, PDF p. 123), so not all of it earns interest for PHE. PHE does not say which balances earn the 251,561.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The murabahah and mudharabah descriptions and PSAK 223's exemption for repetitive inventories are from knowledge, not checked against the standards' text.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of profit or loss and OCI (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 2 basis of preparation, direct method (Daftar 5/29, PDF p. 46); Note 3g financial instruments, SPPI, EIR (Daftar 5/38–5/40 and 5/46–5/47, PDF pp. 55–57 and 63–64); Note 3aa borrowing costs (Daftar 5/76, PDF p. 93); Note 6 (Daftar 5/93, PDF p. 110); Note 7 (Daftar 5/95, PDF p. 112); Note 12 (Daftar 5/109, PDF p. 126); Note 14 hedge ineffectiveness (Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123); Note 20 (Daftar 5/114–5/115, PDF pp. 131–132); Note 25b (Daftar 5/122–5/123, PDF pp. 139–140); Note 27 (Daftar 5/136 and 5/140, PDF pp. 153 and 157); Note 30 (Daftar 5/146, PDF p. 163); Notes 40, 41 and 42 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167); Note 46 segment information (Daftar 5/220, PDF p. 237); Note 50 (Daftar 5/235, PDF p. 252). Totals checked against the PHE FS 2024 data extract. VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 109 (IFRS 9), para. 5.4.1 (gross carrying amount; amortised cost for credit-impaired assets) and Appendix A definition of gross carrying amount: VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation agenda decision \"Curing of a credit-impaired financial asset\", March 2019; IAS Plus meeting notes). Credit-adjusted EIR for purchased credit-impaired assets, dividends: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 201 (IAS 1), para. 82(a): VERIFIED (web, IFRS Interpretations Committee agenda decision \"Presentation of interest revenue for particular financial instruments\", March 2018). Paras. 82(b) and 82(c): VERIFIED (web, IFRS Interpretations Committee July 2013 agenda paper 12, reproducing IAS 1.82). Para. 32: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 118 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Penyajian dan Pengungkapan dalam Laporan Keuangan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IFRS 18): ratified by DSAK IAI 28 May 2025, effective 1 January 2027, replaces PSAK 201, required subtotals: VERIFIED (web, IAI ","type":"text"},{"text":"DSAK Terkini","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" vol. 7 2025; Binus Accounting, Aug 2026; Fortune IDN). Categories and contents of investing and financing for entities without a specified main business: VERIFIED (web, ACCA DipIFR technical article \"IFRS 18\"; BDO). Consequential amendments to IAS 7 (interest and dividends paid → financing, received → investing; operating profit as indirect-method starting point; effective 1 January 2027): VERIFIED (web, KPMG \"IFRS 18 changes to the statement of cash flows\", 2026; EY Applying IFRS 18, 2025–2026).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 207 (IAS 7), paras. 31–33: VERIFIED (web, IFRS staff paper \"Classification of interest paid that is capitalised\", May 2011, quoting the paragraphs; IAS Plus).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 219 (IAS 19): no prescribed presentation of service cost and net interest: VERIFIED (web, secondary: IAI \"IAS 19R Employee Benefits – Indonesia\" training slides).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK numbers 109 (71), 201 (1), 207 (2), 115 (72), 116 (73), 219 (24), 223 (26), 237 (57), 107 (60), ISAK 101 (ISAK 9): from IAI's renumbering rule, as in the sibling essays. PSAK 410 (110 Akuntansi Sukuk), PSAK 402 (102 Murabahah) and PSAK 405 (105 Mudharabah): VERIFIED (web, IAI \"Komparasi Perubahan Penomoran PSAK ISAK SAK Indonesia\"). Content of PSAK 223.6(e), 223.12 and the repetitive-inventory exemption, PSAK 402, PSAK 405, PSAK 107: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", IFRS edition, chs. 4, 14 and 17. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of interest as income and expense, from accrual to coverage, worked through PHE's finance lines and set beside the industries where interest is the business itself","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["Interest is the price of time on money, and the same contract produces interest expense for the borrower and interest income for the lender. Both are measured with the effective interest rate on amortised cost. A lender computes interest income on the gross carrying amount until an asset becomes credit-impaired, and from then on on the amount net of the loss allowance.","Finance cost is an aggregate. It collects interest on borrowings, interest on lease liabilities, the unwinding of discounted provisions and sometimes net interest on employee benefits, less whatever borrowing cost is capitalised into assets. Each piece comes from a different standard.","Where interest lands in profit or loss and in the cash flow statement has been a policy choice under PSAK 201 and PSAK 207. From 1 January 2027 PSAK 118 and its amendments to PSAK 207 remove most of that choice for a non-financial entity: interest on cash and investments goes to the investing category, interest on liabilities to financing, and interest paid and received follow into financing and investing cash flows."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = '072a003d-0fb4-488b-bc5e-ac0fc130e6de' AND updated_at = '2026-10-12T09:47:08.318531+00:00'::timestamptz;
