-- fixed-assets-net-cost-depreciation-impairment
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>Cost is everything needed to bring an asset to the place and condition in which it can work as intended, including borrowing costs on a qualifying asset, and nothing it could work without. Deferred payment, exchanges and basket purchases are all brought back to a cash-price or fair-value equivalent.</p></li><li><p>Depreciation allocates that cost over the asset's useful life in the pattern its benefits are used up. Whether that cost will come back is a separate question for the impairment test under PSAK 236, which, when the carrying amount is higher, writes the asset down to the higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use.</p></li><li><p>After recognition an entity chooses, by class, between the cost model and the revaluation model. Revaluation gains go to other comprehensive income, while gains and losses on disposal go to profit or loss and never count as revenue.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report fixed assets, net, of 544,278 at 31 December 2024 against 522,121 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11). That is 1.8 percent of total assets (1.7 percent a year earlier). The wells and production installations sit on a different line, oil and gas properties, net, which holds 17,905,149, or 58.8 percent of total assets.</p><p>A student should be able to measure, depreciate, impair, revalue and derecognise an asset, with journals.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. The governing standard is PSAK 216 <em>Aset Tetap</em> (formerly PSAK 16, IAS 16).</p><h2>1. What property, plant and equipment is</h2><p>Property, plant and equipment is a tangible asset held for use in production or supply, for rental, or for administration, and expected to be used for more than one period (PSAK 216.6). Kieso's <em>Intermediate Accounting</em> describes it through four characteristics. It has physical form, it is used in operations, it is not held for sale, and it lasts more than one period. Without physical form an item is an intangible asset, held for speculation it is an investment (investment property under PSAK 240, formerly PSAK 13, IAS 40), and held for sale it is inventory.</p><p>An item is recognised when future economic benefits are probable and its cost can be measured reliably (para. 7). Spare parts and stand-by equipment are fixed assets when they meet the definition, and inventory otherwise (para. 8).</p><p>PHE presents wells and installations as oil and gas properties (Note 17) and leased assets as right-of-use assets (Note 18). Its fixed assets line holds land, buildings, machinery, movable assets, steel constructions and construction in progress (Note 12, Daftar 5/100, PDF p. 117).</p><h2>2. Acquisition cost</h2><p>Cost is the purchase price, including import duties and non-refundable taxes, less trade discounts, plus every cost directly attributable to bringing the asset to the location and condition needed for it to operate as management intends, plus the initial estimate of dismantling and restoration (para. 16).</p><ul><li><p><em>Land</em>: price, notary and title transfer, clearing and levelling, encumbrances assumed, permanent improvements, and demolition of an old building net of salvage.</p></li><li><p><em>Buildings</em>: materials, labour and overhead of construction, professional fees and permits.</p></li><li><p><em>Equipment</em>: price, freight, installation, testing and insurance in transit.</p></li></ul><p>Costs the asset could work without are expensed: opening a new facility, training staff, advertising, and general administration (para. 19). Capitalisation stops when the asset is ready for use, even if it is not yet used (para. 20).</p><p>For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary buys a site with an old warehouse to build a fuel depot. Note 12 lists a fuel depot under construction.</p><blockquote><p>Land = 2,400 price + 60 notary and title transfer + 90 demolition − 15 salvage + 45 levelling = 2,580</p><p>Building = 3,200 construction contract + 40 permits + 110 architect's fees = 3,350</p><p>Pumps and tanks = 1,500 × (1 − 2%) trade discount + 35 freight + 50 installation + 15 testing = 1,570</p></blockquote><p>Training the depot staff (20) and the opening ceremony (10) are expensed. Since 2023 in Indonesia (an IAS 16 amendment of 2020), proceeds from anything sold while testing the asset go to profit or loss with the cost of producing it, and are no longer deducted from cost.</p><h2>3. Self-constructed assets</h2><p>An asset built in-house raises a new question, which of the entity's own costs belong to it. Materials and direct labour are traceable. Overhead is the problem, and Kieso gives two approaches. The first assigns no fixed overhead, on the argument that the overhead would exist with or without the construction. The second, <em>full costing</em>, assigns a share of overhead to the asset, as it would to any product.</p><p>PSAK 216 costs a self-constructed asset like a purchased one, at what is directly attributable to bringing it to working condition (paras. 16–17, 22). Only an entity that builds similar assets for sale uses inventory costing under PSAK 202 <em>Inventories</em>. Internal profit and abnormal waste are excluded either way, and so is head-office administration (para. 19). Incremental workshop costs qualify. Fixed workshop overhead is a judgement. Kieso's full costing includes it, and the standard asks only whether it is directly attributable.</p><p>For example (illustration), a subsidiary's workshop builds a hydraulic workover unit (Note 12 lists one under construction). Materials 800, direct labour 300, incremental workshop costs 60, the workshop's fixed overhead allocated on labour hours 140, rework after a welding error 25.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>No fixed overhead</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Full costing</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Materials, labour, incremental costs</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,160</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,160</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Share of fixed workshop overhead</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>expensed</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>140</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Rework (abnormal)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>expensed, 25</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>expensed, 25</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Cost of the unit</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,160</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,300</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The rest of this page uses the full-costing 1,300.</p><h2>4. Borrowing costs during construction</h2><p>Kieso sets out three views of interest during construction: expense it as a financing cost, capitalise interest on all funds including imputed equity, or capitalise only interest actually incurred. Kieso measures the third as <em>avoidable interest</em>, the interest that would have been avoided had the asset not been built. PSAK 223 <em>Biaya Pinjaman</em> (formerly PSAK 26, IAS 23) takes the third view and makes it mandatory. Borrowing costs directly attributable to a <em>qualifying asset</em>, one that necessarily takes a substantial period to get ready for use or sale, are part of its cost.</p><p>Capitalisation starts when spending, borrowing and construction have all begun, pauses during long stoppages, and ends when the work is substantially complete. The amount has two parts.</p><ul><li><p><em>Specific borrowings</em>: the actual cost of a loan taken for the asset, less income from investing its unspent proceeds.</p></li><li><p><em>General borrowings</em>: for spending financed from the general pool, the <em>capitalisation rate</em>, the weighted-average rate on general borrowings, applied to that spending, capped at the general interest actually incurred.</p></li></ul><p>For example (illustration), the fuel depot is built during 2025. Payments are 1,200 on 1 January, 1,800 on 1 April, 1,500 on 1 September and 900 on 31 December. Weight each by the months it was tied up.</p><blockquote><p>Weighted-average accumulated expenditure = 1,200 × 12/12 + 1,800 × 9/12 + 1,500 × 4/12 + 900 × 0 = 3,050</p></blockquote><p>A specific loan of 2,000 at 8 percent is drawn on 1 January. 1,200 is spent that day and the other 800 sits on deposit at 4 percent until 1 April.</p><blockquote><p>Specific borrowing cost = 2,000 × 8% − 800 × 4% × 3/12 = 160 − 8 = 152</p><p>Spending funded by the loan = 1,200 × 12/12 + 800 × 9/12 = 1,800</p></blockquote><p>General borrowings are a bond of 10,000 at 6 percent and a bank loan of 5,000 at 9 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Capitalisation rate = (600 + 450) / 15,000 = 7.0%</p><p>General borrowing cost = (3,050 − 1,800) × 7.0% = 87.5, below the 1,050 incurred</p><p>Borrowing costs capitalised = 152 + 87.5 = 239.5</p></blockquote><p>Kieso's illustration deducts the whole 2,000 from weighted expenditure, (3,050 − 2,000) × 7.0% = 73.5, so 152 + 73.5 = 225.5. That treats the idle 800 as funding the depot from January while it earned deposit income. Tracing gives the loan 1,800 and the general pool 1,250. The difference of 14 (200 × 7%) vanishes when a loan is spent as it is drawn. PSAK 223 prescribes neither split, so the tracing is this page's reading.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Account</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Debit</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Credit</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Construction in progress, fuel depot</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>247.5</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest expense</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>962.5</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash (160 + 1,050 interest paid)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,210.0</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash (deposit income)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>8.0</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Construction in progress, fuel depot</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>8.0</p></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>5. Valuing acquisitions that are not plain cash purchases</h2><p>An asset is recorded at its cash price equivalent or at fair value (paras. 23–24), whatever the form of payment.</p><p><strong>Cash discounts.</strong> Kieso favours deducting a cash discount from cost whether it is taken or not, because the cash price is the real cost and a missed discount is inefficiency. The alternative records cost at the gross price when the discount is missed. PSAK 216 does not settle the choice. Its interest rule (para. 23) covers payment deferred beyond normal credit terms, and day 30 of net 30 is within them. For example (illustration), a tanker truck listed at 150 on terms 2/10, net 30.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Discount reduces cost either way</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Gross when discount missed</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Paid within 10 days</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Vehicles 147 / Cr Cash 147</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Vehicles 147 / Cr Cash 147</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Paid on day 30</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Vehicles 147, Dr Discounts lost 3 / Cr Cash 150</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Vehicles 150 / Cr Cash 150</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Deferred payment.</strong> When payment runs beyond normal credit terms, cost is the present value of the payments and the rest is interest over the credit period (para. 23). For example (illustration, Kieso's zero-interest-bearing note case), equipment is bought for five annual payments of 200 when the market rate is 10 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Present value = 200 × 3.79079 = 758.16 (Dr Equipment / Cr Notes payable)</p><p>Year 1: interest = 10% × 758.16 = 75.82; principal repaid = 200 − 75.82 = 124.18</p><p>Year 2: interest = 10% × (758.16 − 124.18) = 63.40; principal repaid = 136.60</p></blockquote><p><strong>Basket purchases.</strong> One price for several assets is split by relative fair value. For example (illustration), land, a building and equipment bought together for 3,000 with fair values of 1,200, 1,800 and 600 (total 3,600) take 3,000 × 1,200/3,600 = 1,000, 1,500 and 500.</p><p><strong>Exchanges.</strong> An asset received for a non-monetary asset is measured at fair value unless the exchange lacks <em>commercial substance</em> or neither fair value is reliable, in which case it takes the carrying amount of the asset given up (para. 24). An exchange has commercial substance when the risk, timing or amount of the cash flows changes significantly (para. 25). For example (illustration), a subsidiary trades an old workover unit (cost 1,000, accumulated depreciation 700, carrying amount 300, fair value 380) plus 600 cash for a new one.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Commercial substance</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>No commercial substance</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>New unit</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>380 + 600 = 980</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>300 + 600 = 900</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Journal</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr New unit 980, Dr Acc. depreciation 700 / Cr Old unit 1,000, Cr Cash 600, Cr Gain 80</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr New unit 900, Dr Acc. depreciation 700 / Cr Old unit 1,000, Cr Cash 600</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The rule stops entities manufacturing gains by swapping similar assets.</p><h2>6. Costs after acquisition</h2><p>Later spending passes the same test as the original cost. It is capitalised if it adds future economic benefit and expensed if it keeps the asset working as before. Kieso sorts it into three groups.</p><ul><li><p><em>Additions</em> create something new, such as a second loading bay, and are capitalised.</p></li><li><p><em>Improvements and replacements</em> swap a part for a better or a like one. The new part is capitalised and the carrying amount of the old part is removed, whether or not it was depreciated separately (paras. 13, 70).</p></li><li><p><em>Repairs</em>. Ordinary repairs, minor parts, repainting and cleaning, are expensed (para. 12). A major inspection that is a condition of operating, such as an aircraft overhaul every four years, is capitalised and depreciated until the next one (para. 14).</p></li></ul><p>PHE's policy states the same rules (Note 3i, Daftar 5/52, PDF p. 69). For example (illustration), the workover unit's engine, originally 200 of its 1,300 cost and 60 percent depreciated, is replaced for 260, and a routine service costs 15.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Workover unit 260 / Cr Cash 260</p><p>Dr Accumulated depreciation 120, Dr Loss on replacement 80 / Cr Workover unit 200</p><p>Dr Repairs and maintenance 15 / Cr Cash 15</p></blockquote><h2>7. Depreciation</h2><p>Cost is spent to earn revenue over several periods, so depreciation allocates the <em>depreciable amount</em>, cost less <em>residual value</em> (what the asset would fetch now if it were already at the end of its life), over the <em>useful life</em>, the period the entity expects to use it. Three estimates drive it.</p><ul><li><p><em>Useful life</em> reflects expected use, physical wear, technical or commercial obsolescence, and legal limits such as lease expiry (para. 56). Land is not depreciated, because its life is unlimited (para. 58).</p></li><li><p><em>Residual value</em>, reviewed at least each year end.</p></li><li><p><em>Method</em>, which must reflect the pattern in which the benefits are consumed (para. 60). Straight-line, diminishing balance and units of production are all allowed (para. 62). Methods based on revenue are not, because revenue reflects prices as well as use (2014 amendment).</p></li></ul><p>Depreciation starts when the asset is available for use and does not stop when it sits idle, unless a usage method gives a nil charge (para. 55).</p><p>For example (illustration), the workover unit costs 1,300, has a residual value of 100 and a five-year life, and is expected to run 20,000 hours: 5,000, 4,500, 4,000, 3,500 and 3,000.</p><blockquote><p>Straight-line = (1,300 − 100) / 5 = 240 a year</p><p>Units of activity = 1,200 / 20,000 = 0.06 per hour</p><p>Sum-of-the-years'-digits: fractions 5/15, 4/15 ... 1/15 of 1,200</p><p>Double-declining balance: 2 / 5 = 40% of opening carrying amount; year 5 takes the balance down to the residual value, 168.48 − 100 = 68.48</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 125px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Year</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Straight-line</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Units of activity</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Sum-of-years'-digits</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Double-declining</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>240</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>300</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>400</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>520.00</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>240</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>270</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>320</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>312.00</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>240</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>240</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>240</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>187.20</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>240</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>210</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>160</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>112.32</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>5</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>240</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>180</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>80</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>68.48</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,200</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,200</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,200</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,200.00</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Every method spends the same 1,200, so the choice must follow how the asset is used.</p><p>Each part with a significant cost, such as the 200 engine above, is depreciated separately as a <em>component</em> (para. 43). An asset bought on 1 April is depreciated for nine months in its first year, 240 × 9/12 = 180 on straight-line.</p><p><strong>Revisions.</strong> A change in life, residual value or method is a change in estimate under PSAK 208 (formerly PSAK 25, IAS 8), applied from now on without restating the past (para. 61). For example (illustration), after two years of straight-line the carrying amount is 1,300 − 480 = 820. If the remaining life is revised to four years and the residual value to 60, depreciation becomes (820 − 60) / 4 = 190 a year.</p><p>PHE uses straight-line over 4–20 years for buildings, 2–10 for machinery, 2–5 for office and transport equipment and 10 for steel construction, reviewed each year end (Note 3i, PDF pp. 69–70). The table has no line for movable assets, the largest class.</p><h2>8. Impairment</h2><p>Depreciation spreads cost, and a separate test asks whether the cost will come back. PSAK 236 <em>Penurunan Nilai Aset</em> (formerly PSAK 48, IAS 36) does that. At each reporting date the entity looks for indications of impairment, external (falling prices, rising interest rates) or internal (damage, idleness, plans to dispose). If there is one, it estimates the <em>recoverable amount</em>, the higher of two measures.</p><ul><li><p><em>Fair value less costs of disposal</em>: what a buyer would pay, net of selling costs.</p></li><li><p><em>Value in use</em>: the present value of the cash the asset will generate in the entity's hands.</p></li></ul><p>If the carrying amount exceeds the recoverable amount, the difference is an impairment loss in profit or loss. An asset that does not generate cash on its own is tested within its <em>cash-generating unit</em>, the smallest group of assets with largely independent cash inflows.</p><p>For example (illustration), a fleet of fuel tanker trucks loses its main transport contract. Carrying amount 900, remaining life four years, no residual value. A dealer would pay 520 net of costs; the trucks' expected cash flows on the remaining jobs have a present value of 610.</p><blockquote><p>Recoverable amount = higher of 520 and 610 = 610; impairment loss = 900 − 610 = 290</p><p>Dr Impairment loss 290 / Cr Allowance for impairment 290</p><p>New depreciation = 610 / 4 = 152.5 a year</p></blockquote><p><strong>Reversal.</strong> If the estimates later improve, the loss is reversed, but only up to the carrying amount the asset would have had with no impairment (PHE's Note 3w, Daftar 5/73, PDF p. 90). Two years on, a new contract lifts the recoverable amount to 500.</p><blockquote><p>Carrying amount after two years = 610 − 2 × 152.5 = 305</p><p>Ceiling = 900 − 2 × 225 = 450, so reversal = 450 − 305 = 145 (the full 500 − 305 = 195 is capped)</p><p>Dr Allowance for impairment 145 / Cr Reversal of impairment loss 145</p></blockquote><p>PHE's fixed assets carried an allowance for impairment of 5,383 at the start of 2024. It recognised 117 of new impairment and a correction of 51, ending at 5,449 (Note 12, Daftar 5/100–5/101, PDF pp. 117–118).</p><h2>9. The revaluation model</h2><p>After recognition an entity chooses, for each class of assets, the cost model or the revaluation model (para. 29). Under revaluation the asset is carried at fair value at the revaluation date less later depreciation and impairment, and revaluations must be frequent enough that the carrying amount stays close to fair value (para. 34). The whole class is revalued together, so that the balance sheet does not mix costs and values of different dates (paras. 36, 38).</p><p>An increase goes to other comprehensive income and accumulates in equity as <em>revaluation surplus</em>, except that it reverses an earlier decrease on the same asset that went to profit or loss. A decrease goes to profit or loss, except to the extent of surplus already held for that asset (paras. 39–40). At the revaluation date either the gross amount is restated consistently with the new carrying amount, for example in proportion, and accumulated depreciation becomes the difference, or accumulated depreciation is eliminated (para. 35). For example (illustration), a building with cost 1,000 and accumulated depreciation 400 is revalued to 840.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Elimination</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Proportional restatement</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Ratio</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>840 / 600 = 1.4</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Journal</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Acc. depreciation 400 / Cr Building 400; Dr Building 240 / Cr Revaluation surplus 240</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Building 400 / Cr Acc. depreciation 160, Cr Revaluation surplus 240</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>After</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Building 840, acc. depreciation 0</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Building 1,400, acc. depreciation 560</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Both carry 840 and a surplus of 240. With twelve years left, depreciation becomes 840 / 12 = 70 a year instead of 600 / 12 = 50 on cost. The entity may transfer the 20 difference each year from revaluation surplus to retained earnings, or the whole surplus on disposal, but never through profit or loss (para. 41). Deferred tax on the surplus follows PSAK 212 (formerly PSAK 46, IAS 12) (para. 42).</p><p>PHE uses the cost model but discloses appraisals. Fixed assets with a carrying amount of 55,934 were appraised by independent valuers at 96,246, a Level 3 fair value (Note 12, Daftar 5/103, PDF p. 120). Had PHE revalued that group, ignoring deferred tax and assuming no single asset was worth less than its carrying amount, the line would have risen by 96,246 − 55,934 = 40,312 through other comprehensive income, and the rest of each class would have had to be revalued too.</p><h2>10. Disposals</h2><p>An asset is derecognised when sold or when no future benefit is expected from it. After depreciation is brought up to date, the gain or loss is net proceeds less carrying amount, in profit or loss and outside revenue (paras. 67–71).</p><p>PHE sold or disposed of fixed assets with a net book value of 65 for 43 in 2024, a loss of 22, and reports such gains and losses in other income and expense (Note 12, PDF p. 120; Note 3i, PDF p. 70). For example (illustration), suppose the 65 was one vehicle bought on 1 July 2022 for 130, with no residual value and a five-year life.</p><blockquote><p>Annual depreciation = 130 / 5 = 26; to 31 December 2024 = 26 × 2.5 = 65; carrying amount = 130 − 65 = 65</p><p>Dr Depreciation expense 26 / Cr Accumulated depreciation 26 (for 2024)</p><p>Dr Cash 43, Dr Accumulated depreciation 65, Dr Loss on disposal 22 / Cr Vehicles 130</p></blockquote><h2>11. Fixed assets across industries</h2><p>Several industries apply PSAK 216 in a particular way, or move the asset to another standard.</p><p><strong>Oil and gas.</strong> Exploration and evaluation costs follow PSAK 106 (formerly PSAK 64, IFRS 6) until a field is commercial. PHE depletes its production assets on units of production over proved reserves (Note 3m, Daftar 5/57, PDF p. 74).</p><p><strong>Plantations.</strong> Living plants that only bear produce, such as oil palm, rubber and tea, are <em>bearer plants</em>. Since 1 January 2018 in Indonesia they have been fixed assets under PSAK 216, while the fruit on them stays under PSAK 241 <em>Agriculture</em> (formerly PSAK 69, IAS 41) until harvest. An immature plantation collects its costs like construction in progress and starts depreciating at maturity. For example (illustration), 3,000 of planting and upkeep to maturity over a 20-year productive life gives 3,000 / 20 = 150 a year.</p><p><strong>Utilities and revaluation.</strong> PLN, the state electricity company, revalued its fixed assets in 2015, adding about Rp653.4 trillion and so raising its later depreciation (detikFinance, 2016).</p><p><strong>Toll roads and other concessions.</strong> An operator that builds a road under a concession in which the government controls its use and takes it back at the end does not recognise the road as a fixed asset. Under ISAK 112 (formerly ISAK 16, IFRIC 12) it recognises an intangible right to charge users, or a financial asset if the grantor guarantees the cash.</p><p><strong>Land rights in Indonesia.</strong> Indonesian land is held through rights such as <em>hak guna bangunan</em> that run for a term and can be extended. ISAK 336 (formerly ISAK 36) asks whether a right is in substance a purchase of land, a fixed asset that is not depreciated, or a right to use, which is a lease under PSAK 116. PHE's policy applies that test, and puts the initial legal costs of a right into land, undepreciated (Note 3i, PDF p. 69).</p><h2>12. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 12</h2><p>PSAK 216 asks for each class's measurement basis, method, lives and a reconciliation of movements, which PHE gives (Note 12, Daftar 5/100, PDF p. 117).</p><table style="min-width: 50px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 December 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Amount</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cost (of which land 22,782, construction in progress 48,161)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,337,293</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Less accumulated depreciation</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(787,566)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Less allowance for impairment</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(5,449)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Fixed assets, net</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>544,278</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Opening 522,121 + additions 101,072 − disposals (903 − 853) − net correction (17,321 − 12,833) − depreciation 74,311 − impairment 117 + impairment correction 51 = 544,278</p></blockquote><p>The cash flow statement shows 101,071 paid for fixed assets (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17). Construction in progress grew from 31,354 to 48,161, led by rig equipment at 30,558 (Note 12, Daftar 5/102, PDF p. 119).</p><blockquote><p>Accumulated depreciation / depreciable cost = 787,566 / (1,337,293 − 22,782 − 48,161) = 787,566 / 1,266,350 = 62.2%</p></blockquote><p>The assets are more than half used up; 115,950 of cost is fully depreciated but still in use (Note 12, PDF p. 119). PHE does not explain the "correction" column (−17,321 of cost, +12,833 of accumulated depreciation) or why the movement table removes a net 50 on disposal while the disposal note shows 65.</p><h2>13. Upstream note: who owns the equipment</h2><p>Under PHE's cost recovery production sharing contracts, equipment bought for petroleum operations belongs to the Government, and the contractor may use it until SKK Migas, the upstream regulator, approves it as surplus or abandoned (Note 2a(vii), Daftar 5/11, PDF p. 28). Yet Note 12 says all fixed assets are directly owned by the group. One possible reading is that PSC equipment sits in oil and gas properties and this line holds assets of subsidiaries such as PDSI, a drilling contractor, and Elnusa, an oilfield-service company (Note 1b, Daftar 5/5–5/6, PDF pp. 22–23). The FS does not say. PHE capitalised 2,277 of borrowing costs on the JTB project financing in 2024, disclosed in the oil and gas properties note (Note 17, Daftar 5/109, PDF p. 126; policy 3aa, PDF p. 93).</p><h2>14. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net">oil-gas-properties-net</a>: wells, installations and development assets, depreciated on units of production.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/right-of-use-assets-net">right-of-use-assets-net</a>: leased assets and land rights that are in substance leases.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/inventories-net">inventories-net</a>: the boundary for spare parts and stand-by equipment.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net">depreciation-depletion-amortization</a>: where the 74,311 of 2024 depreciation is charged.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>That the fixed assets line holds group-owned assets and PSC equipment sits in oil and gas properties is this page's reading of Note 2a(vii) and Note 12 together. PHE does not say where state-owned PSC equipment is presented.</p></li><li><p>PHE's policy table lists lives for "transportation equipment" and other classes, but Note 12 reports a class called "movable assets" (932,365 of cost) that the table does not name. Its 2024 depreciation of 54,864 on average cost of 898,489 is 6.1 percent, which implies a life far longer than the 2–5 years for transport. This page could not match the two.</p></li><li><p>Including allocated fixed workshop overhead in a self-constructed asset (the full-costing 1,300) is a judgement under PSAK 216.16–17, which ask only whether a cost is directly attributable. Paragraph 22 points to inventory costing only for entities that build similar assets for sale. How Indonesian preparers treat fixed overhead was not researched.</p></li><li><p>Tracing the specific loan to 1,800 of expenditure, rather than deducting the whole 2,000 as Kieso does, is this page's reading of PSAK 223.12–14. The standard does not prescribe the split; the two methods differ here by 14.</p></li><li><p>The ISAK 112 and ISAK 336 summaries were checked against secondary sources and PHE's policy wording; neither interpretation's own text was opened.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 1b (Daftar 5/5–5/6, PDF pp. 22–23); Note 2a(vii) (Daftar 5/11, PDF p. 28); Note 3i (Daftar 5/52–5/53, PDF pp. 69–70); Note 3m (Daftar 5/57, PDF p. 74); Note 3w (Daftar 5/72–5/73, PDF pp. 89–90); Note 3aa (Daftar 5/76, PDF p. 93); Note 12 (Daftar 5/100–5/103, PDF pp. 117–120); Note 17 (Daftar 5/109, PDF p. 126). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).</p></li><li><p>Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting</em>, IFRS edition (Wiley), ch. 10 (acquisition and disposition of PPE) and ch. 11 (depreciation, impairments and depletion; revaluations). Followed for topic order and for the treatments attributed to Kieso in the text. The book itself was not opened for this page; edition not confirmed. TEXTBOOK — NOT OPENED.</p></li><li><p>Kieso's chapter on depreciation, impairment and depletion (Intermediate Accounting IFRS edition, ch. 11). Sections 7–9 follow that chapter's order (depreciation methods, components, revisions, impairment and reversal, revaluation) and the standards.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 216 <em>Aset Tetap</em> (IAS 16), paras. 6–8, 12–14, 16, 19–20, 22–25, 29, 34–43, 55–62, 67–71. VERIFIED (web, IAS 16 text, IFRS Foundation 2013 bound volume, reproduced at ktkt.uel.edu.vn); para. 22 re-checked (web, ICAI EAC opinion and other reproductions). Later amendments checked against the amendment pages, not the 2013 text: para. 35 as amended from 1 July 2014 (ifrs.org, "Revaluation method—proportionate restatement of accumulated depreciation"); revenue-based depreciation (2014) and proceeds before intended use (issued 2020) (ifrs.org IAS 16 page); PSAK 16 proceeds amendment effective 1 January 2023 (PwC Indonesia, <em>Practical guide to PSAK 2023</em>). VERIFIED (web). Revaluation and derecognition also checked against ACCA, "Part 2: Revaluation and derecognition" (web).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 223 <em>Biaya Pinjaman</em> (IAS 23): mandatory capitalisation, commencement, suspension, cessation, cap: VERIFIED (web, PKF "IAS 23 Borrowing Costs"). Paras. 12–14 and 18 (specific and general borrowings, investment income, expenditures): VERIFIED (web, AASB 123 text, standards.aasb.gov.au).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 236 <em>Penurunan Nilai Aset</em> (IAS 36): indicators, recoverable amount, cash-generating units. VERIFIED (web, ICAEW "Applying IAS 36" factsheet; IAS Plus summary listing). Reversal ceiling as stated in PHE Note 3w.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 208 (IAS 8), PSAK 212 (IAS 12), PSAK 106 (IFRS 6), PSAK 240 (IAS 40), PSAK 241 (IAS 41), ISAK 112 (IFRIC 12), ISAK 336. Numbers and titles VERIFIED (web, IAI, <em>Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia</em>). ISAK 336's substance test VERIFIED (web, secondary: Binus Accounting on ISAK 36, 2020); ISAK 112's intangible and financial asset models VERIFIED (web, secondary: IMF GFSAC paper on IFRIC 12). Other content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Bearer plants in PSAK 216 from 1 January 2018: VERIFIED (web, RSM Indonesia, "Akuntansi tanaman perkebunan").</p></li><li><p>PLN revaluation 2015: detikFinance, "Setelah Revaluasi, Aset PLN Menjadi Rp 1.227 Triliun". VERIFIED (web, press report).</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost is everything needed to bring an asset to the place and condition in which it can work as intended, including borrowing costs on a qualifying asset, and nothing it could work without. Deferred payment, exchanges and basket purchases are all brought back to a cash-price or fair-value equivalent.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Depreciation allocates that cost over the asset's useful life in the pattern its benefits are used up. Whether that cost will come back is a separate question for the impairment test under PSAK 236, which, when the carrying amount is higher, writes the asset down to the higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After recognition an entity chooses, by class, between the cost model and the revaluation model. Revaluation gains go to other comprehensive income, while gains and losses on disposal go to profit or loss and never count as revenue.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report fixed assets, net, of 544,278 at 31 December 2024 against 522,121 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11). That is 1.8 percent of total assets (1.7 percent a year earlier). The wells and production installations sit on a different line, oil and gas properties, net, which holds 17,905,149, or 58.8 percent of total assets.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A student should be able to measure, depreciate, impair, revalue and derecognise an asset, with journals.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. The governing standard is PSAK 216 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Aset Tetap","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 16, IAS 16).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. What property, plant and equipment is","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Property, plant and equipment is a tangible asset held for use in production or supply, for rental, or for administration, and expected to be used for more than one period (PSAK 216.6). Kieso's ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" describes it through four characteristics. It has physical form, it is used in operations, it is not held for sale, and it lasts more than one period. Without physical form an item is an intangible asset, held for speculation it is an investment (investment property under PSAK 240, formerly PSAK 13, IAS 40), and held for sale it is inventory.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An item is recognised when future economic benefits are probable and its cost can be measured reliably (para. 7). Spare parts and stand-by equipment are fixed assets when they meet the definition, and inventory otherwise (para. 8).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE presents wells and installations as oil and gas properties (Note 17) and leased assets as right-of-use assets (Note 18). Its fixed assets line holds land, buildings, machinery, movable assets, steel constructions and construction in progress (Note 12, Daftar 5/100, PDF p. 117).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Acquisition cost","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost is the purchase price, including import duties and non-refundable taxes, less trade discounts, plus every cost directly attributable to bringing the asset to the location and condition needed for it to operate as management intends, plus the initial estimate of dismantling and restoration (para. 16).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Land","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": price, notary and title transfer, clearing and levelling, encumbrances assumed, permanent improvements, and demolition of an old building net of salvage.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Buildings","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": materials, labour and overhead of construction, professional fees and permits.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Equipment","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": price, freight, installation, testing and insurance in transit.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Costs the asset could work without are expensed: opening a new facility, training staff, advertising, and general administration (para. 19). Capitalisation stops when the asset is ready for use, even if it is not yet used (para. 20).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary buys a site with an old warehouse to build a fuel depot. Note 12 lists a fuel depot under construction.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Land = 2,400 price + 60 notary and title transfer + 90 demolition − 15 salvage + 45 levelling = 2,580","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Building = 3,200 construction contract + 40 permits + 110 architect's fees = 3,350","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Pumps and tanks = 1,500 × (1 − 2%) trade discount + 35 freight + 50 installation + 15 testing = 1,570","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Training the depot staff (20) and the opening ceremony (10) are expensed. Since 2023 in Indonesia (an IAS 16 amendment of 2020), proceeds from anything sold while testing the asset go to profit or loss with the cost of producing it, and are no longer deducted from cost.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Self-constructed assets","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An asset built in-house raises a new question, which of the entity's own costs belong to it. Materials and direct labour are traceable. Overhead is the problem, and Kieso gives two approaches. The first assigns no fixed overhead, on the argument that the overhead would exist with or without the construction. The second, ","type":"text"},{"text":"full costing","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", assigns a share of overhead to the asset, as it would to any product.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 216 costs a self-constructed asset like a purchased one, at what is directly attributable to bringing it to working condition (paras. 16–17, 22). Only an entity that builds similar assets for sale uses inventory costing under PSAK 202 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Inventories","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". Internal profit and abnormal waste are excluded either way, and so is head-office administration (para. 19). Incremental workshop costs qualify. Fixed workshop overhead is a judgement. Kieso's full costing includes it, and the standard asks only whether it is directly attributable.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a subsidiary's workshop builds a hydraulic workover unit (Note 12 lists one under construction). Materials 800, direct labour 300, incremental workshop costs 60, the workshop's fixed overhead allocated on labour hours 140, rework after a welding error 25.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"No fixed overhead","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Full costing","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Materials, labour, incremental costs","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,160","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,160","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Share of fixed workshop overhead","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"expensed","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"140","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Rework (abnormal)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"expensed, 25","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"expensed, 25","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost of the unit","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,160","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,300","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The rest of this page uses the full-costing 1,300.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Borrowing costs during construction","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso sets out three views of interest during construction: expense it as a financing cost, capitalise interest on all funds including imputed equity, or capitalise only interest actually incurred. Kieso measures the third as ","type":"text"},{"text":"avoidable interest","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the interest that would have been avoided had the asset not been built. PSAK 223 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Biaya Pinjaman","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 26, IAS 23) takes the third view and makes it mandatory. Borrowing costs directly attributable to a ","type":"text"},{"text":"qualifying asset","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", one that necessarily takes a substantial period to get ready for use or sale, are part of its cost.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Capitalisation starts when spending, borrowing and construction have all begun, pauses during long stoppages, and ends when the work is substantially complete. The amount has two parts.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Specific borrowings","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": the actual cost of a loan taken for the asset, less income from investing its unspent proceeds.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"General borrowings","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": for spending financed from the general pool, the ","type":"text"},{"text":"capitalisation rate","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the weighted-average rate on general borrowings, applied to that spending, capped at the general interest actually incurred.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), the fuel depot is built during 2025. Payments are 1,200 on 1 January, 1,800 on 1 April, 1,500 on 1 September and 900 on 31 December. Weight each by the months it was tied up.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Weighted-average accumulated expenditure = 1,200 × 12/12 + 1,800 × 9/12 + 1,500 × 4/12 + 900 × 0 = 3,050","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A specific loan of 2,000 at 8 percent is drawn on 1 January. 1,200 is spent that day and the other 800 sits on deposit at 4 percent until 1 April.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Specific borrowing cost = 2,000 × 8% − 800 × 4% × 3/12 = 160 − 8 = 152","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Spending funded by the loan = 1,200 × 12/12 + 800 × 9/12 = 1,800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"General borrowings are a bond of 10,000 at 6 percent and a bank loan of 5,000 at 9 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Capitalisation rate = (600 + 450) / 15,000 = 7.0%","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"General borrowing cost = (3,050 − 1,800) × 7.0% = 87.5, below the 1,050 incurred","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Borrowing costs capitalised = 152 + 87.5 = 239.5","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso's illustration deducts the whole 2,000 from weighted expenditure, (3,050 − 2,000) × 7.0% = 73.5, so 152 + 73.5 = 225.5. That treats the idle 800 as funding the depot from January while it earned deposit income. Tracing gives the loan 1,800 and the general pool 1,250. The difference of 14 (200 × 7%) vanishes when a loan is spent as it is drawn. PSAK 223 prescribes neither split, so the tracing is this page's reading.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Account","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Debit","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Credit","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Construction in progress, fuel depot","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"247.5","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest expense","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"962.5","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash (160 + 1,050 interest paid)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,210.0","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash (deposit income)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8.0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Construction in progress, fuel depot","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8.0","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Valuing acquisitions that are not plain cash purchases","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An asset is recorded at its cash price equivalent or at fair value (paras. 23–24), whatever the form of payment.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash discounts.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Kieso favours deducting a cash discount from cost whether it is taken or not, because the cash price is the real cost and a missed discount is inefficiency. The alternative records cost at the gross price when the discount is missed. PSAK 216 does not settle the choice. Its interest rule (para. 23) covers payment deferred beyond normal credit terms, and day 30 of net 30 is within them. For example (illustration), a tanker truck listed at 150 on terms 2/10, net 30.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Discount reduces cost either way","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gross when discount missed","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Paid within 10 days","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Vehicles 147 / Cr Cash 147","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Vehicles 147 / Cr Cash 147","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Paid on day 30","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Vehicles 147, Dr Discounts lost 3 / Cr Cash 150","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Vehicles 150 / Cr Cash 150","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Deferred payment.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" When payment runs beyond normal credit terms, cost is the present value of the payments and the rest is interest over the credit period (para. 23). For example (illustration, Kieso's zero-interest-bearing note case), equipment is bought for five annual payments of 200 when the market rate is 10 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Present value = 200 × 3.79079 = 758.16 (Dr Equipment / Cr Notes payable)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 1: interest = 10% × 758.16 = 75.82; principal repaid = 200 − 75.82 = 124.18","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 2: interest = 10% × (758.16 − 124.18) = 63.40; principal repaid = 136.60","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Basket purchases.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" One price for several assets is split by relative fair value. For example (illustration), land, a building and equipment bought together for 3,000 with fair values of 1,200, 1,800 and 600 (total 3,600) take 3,000 × 1,200/3,600 = 1,000, 1,500 and 500.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Exchanges.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" An asset received for a non-monetary asset is measured at fair value unless the exchange lacks ","type":"text"},{"text":"commercial substance","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" or neither fair value is reliable, in which case it takes the carrying amount of the asset given up (para. 24). An exchange has commercial substance when the risk, timing or amount of the cash flows changes significantly (para. 25). For example (illustration), a subsidiary trades an old workover unit (cost 1,000, accumulated depreciation 700, carrying amount 300, fair value 380) plus 600 cash for a new one.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Commercial substance","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"No commercial substance","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"New unit","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"380 + 600 = 980","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"300 + 600 = 900","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Journal","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr New unit 980, Dr Acc. depreciation 700 / Cr Old unit 1,000, Cr Cash 600, Cr Gain 80","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr New unit 900, Dr Acc. depreciation 700 / Cr Old unit 1,000, Cr Cash 600","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The rule stops entities manufacturing gains by swapping similar assets.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Costs after acquisition","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Later spending passes the same test as the original cost. It is capitalised if it adds future economic benefit and expensed if it keeps the asset working as before. Kieso sorts it into three groups.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Additions","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" create something new, such as a second loading bay, and are capitalised.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Improvements and replacements","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" swap a part for a better or a like one. The new part is capitalised and the carrying amount of the old part is removed, whether or not it was depreciated separately (paras. 13, 70).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Repairs","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". Ordinary repairs, minor parts, repainting and cleaning, are expensed (para. 12). A major inspection that is a condition of operating, such as an aircraft overhaul every four years, is capitalised and depreciated until the next one (para. 14).","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's policy states the same rules (Note 3i, Daftar 5/52, PDF p. 69). For example (illustration), the workover unit's engine, originally 200 of its 1,300 cost and 60 percent depreciated, is replaced for 260, and a routine service costs 15.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Workover unit 260 / Cr Cash 260","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Accumulated depreciation 120, Dr Loss on replacement 80 / Cr Workover unit 200","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Repairs and maintenance 15 / Cr Cash 15","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Depreciation","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost is spent to earn revenue over several periods, so depreciation allocates the ","type":"text"},{"text":"depreciable amount","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", cost less ","type":"text"},{"text":"residual value","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (what the asset would fetch now if it were already at the end of its life), over the ","type":"text"},{"text":"useful life","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the period the entity expects to use it. Three estimates drive it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Useful life","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" reflects expected use, physical wear, technical or commercial obsolescence, and legal limits such as lease expiry (para. 56). Land is not depreciated, because its life is unlimited (para. 58).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Residual value","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", reviewed at least each year end.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", which must reflect the pattern in which the benefits are consumed (para. 60). Straight-line, diminishing balance and units of production are all allowed (para. 62). Methods based on revenue are not, because revenue reflects prices as well as use (2014 amendment).","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Depreciation starts when the asset is available for use and does not stop when it sits idle, unless a usage method gives a nil charge (para. 55).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), the workover unit costs 1,300, has a residual value of 100 and a five-year life, and is expected to run 20,000 hours: 5,000, 4,500, 4,000, 3,500 and 3,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Straight-line = (1,300 − 100) / 5 = 240 a year","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Units of activity = 1,200 / 20,000 = 0.06 per hour","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Sum-of-the-years'-digits: fractions 5/15, 4/15 ... 1/15 of 1,200","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Double-declining balance: 2 / 5 = 40% of opening carrying amount; year 5 takes the balance down to the residual value, 168.48 − 100 = 68.48","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Straight-line","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Units of activity","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Sum-of-years'-digits","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Double-declining","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"240","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"300","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"400","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"520.00","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"240","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"270","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"320","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"312.00","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"240","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"240","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"240","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"187.20","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"240","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"210","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"160","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"112.32","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"240","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"180","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"80","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"68.48","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,200","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,200","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,200","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,200.00","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Every method spends the same 1,200, so the choice must follow how the asset is used.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Each part with a significant cost, such as the 200 engine above, is depreciated separately as a ","type":"text"},{"text":"component","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (para. 43). An asset bought on 1 April is depreciated for nine months in its first year, 240 × 9/12 = 180 on straight-line.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Revisions.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A change in life, residual value or method is a change in estimate under PSAK 208 (formerly PSAK 25, IAS 8), applied from now on without restating the past (para. 61). For example (illustration), after two years of straight-line the carrying amount is 1,300 − 480 = 820. If the remaining life is revised to four years and the residual value to 60, depreciation becomes (820 − 60) / 4 = 190 a year.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE uses straight-line over 4–20 years for buildings, 2–10 for machinery, 2–5 for office and transport equipment and 10 for steel construction, reviewed each year end (Note 3i, PDF pp. 69–70). The table has no line for movable assets, the largest class.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Impairment","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Depreciation spreads cost, and a separate test asks whether the cost will come back. PSAK 236 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Penurunan Nilai Aset","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 48, IAS 36) does that. At each reporting date the entity looks for indications of impairment, external (falling prices, rising interest rates) or internal (damage, idleness, plans to dispose). If there is one, it estimates the ","type":"text"},{"text":"recoverable amount","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the higher of two measures.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fair value less costs of disposal","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": what a buyer would pay, net of selling costs.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Value in use","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": the present value of the cash the asset will generate in the entity's hands.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"If the carrying amount exceeds the recoverable amount, the difference is an impairment loss in profit or loss. An asset that does not generate cash on its own is tested within its ","type":"text"},{"text":"cash-generating unit","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the smallest group of assets with largely independent cash inflows.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a fleet of fuel tanker trucks loses its main transport contract. Carrying amount 900, remaining life four years, no residual value. A dealer would pay 520 net of costs; the trucks' expected cash flows on the remaining jobs have a present value of 610.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Recoverable amount = higher of 520 and 610 = 610; impairment loss = 900 − 610 = 290","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Impairment loss 290 / Cr Allowance for impairment 290","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"New depreciation = 610 / 4 = 152.5 a year","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Reversal.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" If the estimates later improve, the loss is reversed, but only up to the carrying amount the asset would have had with no impairment (PHE's Note 3w, Daftar 5/73, PDF p. 90). Two years on, a new contract lifts the recoverable amount to 500.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Carrying amount after two years = 610 − 2 × 152.5 = 305","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Ceiling = 900 − 2 × 225 = 450, so reversal = 450 − 305 = 145 (the full 500 − 305 = 195 is capped)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Allowance for impairment 145 / Cr Reversal of impairment loss 145","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's fixed assets carried an allowance for impairment of 5,383 at the start of 2024. It recognised 117 of new impairment and a correction of 51, ending at 5,449 (Note 12, Daftar 5/100–5/101, PDF pp. 117–118).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. The revaluation model","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After recognition an entity chooses, for each class of assets, the cost model or the revaluation model (para. 29). Under revaluation the asset is carried at fair value at the revaluation date less later depreciation and impairment, and revaluations must be frequent enough that the carrying amount stays close to fair value (para. 34). The whole class is revalued together, so that the balance sheet does not mix costs and values of different dates (paras. 36, 38).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An increase goes to other comprehensive income and accumulates in equity as ","type":"text"},{"text":"revaluation surplus","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", except that it reverses an earlier decrease on the same asset that went to profit or loss. A decrease goes to profit or loss, except to the extent of surplus already held for that asset (paras. 39–40). At the revaluation date either the gross amount is restated consistently with the new carrying amount, for example in proportion, and accumulated depreciation becomes the difference, or accumulated depreciation is eliminated (para. 35). For example (illustration), a building with cost 1,000 and accumulated depreciation 400 is revalued to 840.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Elimination","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Proportional restatement","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Ratio","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"840 / 600 = 1.4","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Journal","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Acc. depreciation 400 / Cr Building 400; Dr Building 240 / Cr Revaluation surplus 240","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Building 400 / Cr Acc. depreciation 160, Cr Revaluation surplus 240","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Building 840, acc. depreciation 0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Building 1,400, acc. depreciation 560","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Both carry 840 and a surplus of 240. With twelve years left, depreciation becomes 840 / 12 = 70 a year instead of 600 / 12 = 50 on cost. The entity may transfer the 20 difference each year from revaluation surplus to retained earnings, or the whole surplus on disposal, but never through profit or loss (para. 41). Deferred tax on the surplus follows PSAK 212 (formerly PSAK 46, IAS 12) (para. 42).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE uses the cost model but discloses appraisals. Fixed assets with a carrying amount of 55,934 were appraised by independent valuers at 96,246, a Level 3 fair value (Note 12, Daftar 5/103, PDF p. 120). Had PHE revalued that group, ignoring deferred tax and assuming no single asset was worth less than its carrying amount, the line would have risen by 96,246 − 55,934 = 40,312 through other comprehensive income, and the rest of each class would have had to be revalued too.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Disposals","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An asset is derecognised when sold or when no future benefit is expected from it. After depreciation is brought up to date, the gain or loss is net proceeds less carrying amount, in profit or loss and outside revenue (paras. 67–71).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE sold or disposed of fixed assets with a net book value of 65 for 43 in 2024, a loss of 22, and reports such gains and losses in other income and expense (Note 12, PDF p. 120; Note 3i, PDF p. 70). For example (illustration), suppose the 65 was one vehicle bought on 1 July 2022 for 130, with no residual value and a five-year life.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Annual depreciation = 130 / 5 = 26; to 31 December 2024 = 26 × 2.5 = 65; carrying amount = 130 − 65 = 65","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Depreciation expense 26 / Cr Accumulated depreciation 26 (for 2024)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 43, Dr Accumulated depreciation 65, Dr Loss on disposal 22 / Cr Vehicles 130","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11. Fixed assets across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Several industries apply PSAK 216 in a particular way, or move the asset to another standard.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Oil and gas.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Exploration and evaluation costs follow PSAK 106 (formerly PSAK 64, IFRS 6) until a field is commercial. PHE depletes its production assets on units of production over proved reserves (Note 3m, Daftar 5/57, PDF p. 74).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Plantations.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Living plants that only bear produce, such as oil palm, rubber and tea, are ","type":"text"},{"text":"bearer plants","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". Since 1 January 2018 in Indonesia they have been fixed assets under PSAK 216, while the fruit on them stays under PSAK 241 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Agriculture","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 69, IAS 41) until harvest. An immature plantation collects its costs like construction in progress and starts depreciating at maturity. For example (illustration), 3,000 of planting and upkeep to maturity over a 20-year productive life gives 3,000 / 20 = 150 a year.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Utilities and revaluation.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PLN, the state electricity company, revalued its fixed assets in 2015, adding about Rp653.4 trillion and so raising its later depreciation (detikFinance, 2016).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Toll roads and other concessions.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" An operator that builds a road under a concession in which the government controls its use and takes it back at the end does not recognise the road as a fixed asset. Under ISAK 112 (formerly ISAK 16, IFRIC 12) it recognises an intangible right to charge users, or a financial asset if the grantor guarantees the cash.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Land rights in Indonesia.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Indonesian land is held through rights such as ","type":"text"},{"text":"hak guna bangunan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" that run for a term and can be extended. ISAK 336 (formerly ISAK 36) asks whether a right is in substance a purchase of land, a fixed asset that is not depreciated, or a right to use, which is a lease under PSAK 116. PHE's policy applies that test, and puts the initial legal costs of a right into land, undepreciated (Note 3i, PDF p. 69).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 12","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 216 asks for each class's measurement basis, method, lives and a reconciliation of movements, which PHE gives (Note 12, Daftar 5/100, PDF p. 117).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 December 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Amount","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost (of which land 22,782, construction in progress 48,161)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,337,293","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Less accumulated depreciation","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(787,566)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Less allowance for impairment","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(5,449)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fixed assets, net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"544,278","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Opening 522,121 + additions 101,072 − disposals (903 − 853) − net correction (17,321 − 12,833) − depreciation 74,311 − impairment 117 + impairment correction 51 = 544,278","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The cash flow statement shows 101,071 paid for fixed assets (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17). Construction in progress grew from 31,354 to 48,161, led by rig equipment at 30,558 (Note 12, Daftar 5/102, PDF p. 119).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accumulated depreciation / depreciable cost = 787,566 / (1,337,293 − 22,782 − 48,161) = 787,566 / 1,266,350 = 62.2%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The assets are more than half used up; 115,950 of cost is fully depreciated but still in use (Note 12, PDF p. 119). PHE does not explain the \"correction\" column (−17,321 of cost, +12,833 of accumulated depreciation) or why the movement table removes a net 50 on disposal while the disposal note shows 65.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"13. Upstream note: who owns the equipment","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Under PHE's cost recovery production sharing contracts, equipment bought for petroleum operations belongs to the Government, and the contractor may use it until SKK Migas, the upstream regulator, approves it as surplus or abandoned (Note 2a(vii), Daftar 5/11, PDF p. 28). Yet Note 12 says all fixed assets are directly owned by the group. One possible reading is that PSC equipment sits in oil and gas properties and this line holds assets of subsidiaries such as PDSI, a drilling contractor, and Elnusa, an oilfield-service company (Note 1b, Daftar 5/5–5/6, PDF pp. 22–23). The FS does not say. PHE capitalised 2,277 of borrowing costs on the JTB project financing in 2024, disclosed in the oil and gas properties note (Note 17, Daftar 5/109, PDF p. 126; policy 3aa, PDF p. 93).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"14. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"oil-gas-properties-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": wells, installations and development assets, depreciated on units of production.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"right-of-use-assets-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/right-of-use-assets-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": leased assets and land rights that are in substance leases.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"inventories-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/inventories-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the boundary for spare parts and stand-by equipment.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"depreciation-depletion-amortization","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where the 74,311 of 2024 depreciation is charged.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That the fixed assets line holds group-owned assets and PSC equipment sits in oil and gas properties is this page's reading of Note 2a(vii) and Note 12 together. PHE does not say where state-owned PSC equipment is presented.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's policy table lists lives for \"transportation equipment\" and other classes, but Note 12 reports a class called \"movable assets\" (932,365 of cost) that the table does not name. Its 2024 depreciation of 54,864 on average cost of 898,489 is 6.1 percent, which implies a life far longer than the 2–5 years for transport. This page could not match the two.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Including allocated fixed workshop overhead in a self-constructed asset (the full-costing 1,300) is a judgement under PSAK 216.16–17, which ask only whether a cost is directly attributable. Paragraph 22 points to inventory costing only for entities that build similar assets for sale. How Indonesian preparers treat fixed overhead was not researched.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Tracing the specific loan to 1,800 of expenditure, rather than deducting the whole 2,000 as Kieso does, is this page's reading of PSAK 223.12–14. The standard does not prescribe the split; the two methods differ here by 14.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The ISAK 112 and ISAK 336 summaries were checked against secondary sources and PHE's policy wording; neither interpretation's own text was opened.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 1b (Daftar 5/5–5/6, PDF pp. 22–23); Note 2a(vii) (Daftar 5/11, PDF p. 28); Note 3i (Daftar 5/52–5/53, PDF pp. 69–70); Note 3m (Daftar 5/57, PDF p. 74); Note 3w (Daftar 5/72–5/73, PDF pp. 89–90); Note 3aa (Daftar 5/76, PDF p. 93); Note 12 (Daftar 5/100–5/103, PDF pp. 117–120); Note 17 (Daftar 5/109, PDF p. 126). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", IFRS edition (Wiley), ch. 10 (acquisition and disposition of PPE) and ch. 11 (depreciation, impairments and depletion; revaluations). Followed for topic order and for the treatments attributed to Kieso in the text. The book itself was not opened for this page; edition not confirmed. TEXTBOOK — NOT OPENED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso's chapter on depreciation, impairment and depletion (Intermediate Accounting IFRS edition, ch. 11). Sections 7–9 follow that chapter's order (depreciation methods, components, revisions, impairment and reversal, revaluation) and the standards.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 216 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Aset Tetap","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 16), paras. 6–8, 12–14, 16, 19–20, 22–25, 29, 34–43, 55–62, 67–71. VERIFIED (web, IAS 16 text, IFRS Foundation 2013 bound volume, reproduced at ktkt.uel.edu.vn); para. 22 re-checked (web, ICAI EAC opinion and other reproductions). Later amendments checked against the amendment pages, not the 2013 text: para. 35 as amended from 1 July 2014 (ifrs.org, \"Revaluation method—proportionate restatement of accumulated depreciation\"); revenue-based depreciation (2014) and proceeds before intended use (issued 2020) (ifrs.org IAS 16 page); PSAK 16 proceeds amendment effective 1 January 2023 (PwC Indonesia, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Practical guide to PSAK 2023","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"). VERIFIED (web). Revaluation and derecognition also checked against ACCA, \"Part 2: Revaluation and derecognition\" (web).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 223 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Biaya Pinjaman","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 23): mandatory capitalisation, commencement, suspension, cessation, cap: VERIFIED (web, PKF \"IAS 23 Borrowing Costs\"). Paras. 12–14 and 18 (specific and general borrowings, investment income, expenditures): VERIFIED (web, AASB 123 text, standards.aasb.gov.au).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 236 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Penurunan Nilai Aset","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 36): indicators, recoverable amount, cash-generating units. VERIFIED (web, ICAEW \"Applying IAS 36\" factsheet; IAS Plus summary listing). Reversal ceiling as stated in PHE Note 3w.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 208 (IAS 8), PSAK 212 (IAS 12), PSAK 106 (IFRS 6), PSAK 240 (IAS 40), PSAK 241 (IAS 41), ISAK 112 (IFRIC 12), ISAK 336. Numbers and titles VERIFIED (web, IAI, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"). ISAK 336's substance test VERIFIED (web, secondary: Binus Accounting on ISAK 36, 2020); ISAK 112's intangible and financial asset models VERIFIED (web, secondary: IMF GFSAC paper on IFRIC 12). Other content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Bearer plants in PSAK 216 from 1 January 2018: VERIFIED (web, RSM Indonesia, \"Akuntansi tanaman perkebunan\").","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PLN revaluation 2015: detikFinance, \"Setelah Revaluasi, Aset PLN Menjadi Rp 1.227 Triliun\". VERIFIED (web, press report).","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of property, plant and equipment from acquisition to derecognition, worked through PHE's fixed assets and set beside the industries with their own rules","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["Cost is everything needed to bring an asset to the place and condition in which it can work as intended, including borrowing costs on a qualifying asset, and nothing it could work without. Deferred payment, exchanges and basket purchases are all brought back to a cash-price or fair-value equivalent.","Depreciation allocates that cost over the asset's useful life in the pattern its benefits are used up. Whether that cost will come back is a separate question for the impairment test under PSAK 236, which, when the carrying amount is higher, writes the asset down to the higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use.","After recognition an entity chooses, by class, between the cost model and the revaluation model. Revaluation gains go to other comprehensive income, while gains and losses on disposal go to profit or loss and never count as revenue."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = '5ce9af9a-af6c-4e3f-b924-155969777f74' AND updated_at = '2026-10-12T09:47:08.318531+00:00'::timestamptz;
