-- foreign-exchange-loss-net-functional-currency-monetary-items
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>Every entity first determines its functional currency, the currency of the economic environment that mainly sets its prices and costs, from evidence the standard ranks. Any other currency is foreign. A Jakarta company can be a dollar company, and a Paris one can be too.</p></li><li><p>Foreign-currency transactions are recorded at the spot rate on the transaction date. At each reporting date, monetary items (cash, receivables, payables, provisions to be paid in cash) are retranslated at the closing rate and the difference goes to profit or loss. Non-monetary items carried at cost (inventory, fixed assets, prepayments) keep their historical rate.</p></li><li><p>An exchange loss therefore measures the net monetary position held in a foreign currency and how the rate moved while it was held. A dollar-functional company that holds more rupiah assets than rupiah liabilities loses when the rupiah weakens. Translation of a foreign operation into a presentation currency is a different step that goes to other comprehensive income, and hyperinflation (PSAK 229) adds a restatement before any translation.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report a foreign exchange loss, net, of 4,479 for 2024 against 19,772 for 2023, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of profit or loss and other comprehensive income, Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14; "Daftar" is the printed page). The loss is 0.09 percent of profit before income tax of 4,892,576 (0.44 percent of 4,492,029 in 2023). The line has no note of its own.</p><p>After reading it a student should be able to determine an entity's functional currency, record a foreign-currency transaction from invoice to settlement, sort balances into monetary and non-monetary, and explain why a dollar company reports a gain or loss when the rupiah moves.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated; rupiah amounts are in millions. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.</p><h2>1. Three currencies, three questions</h2><p>A group that buys in rupiah, sells in dollars and reports to a parent abroad needs a rule for which currency its books measure in. PSAK 221 <em>The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates</em> (formerly PSAK 10, IAS 21) separates three currencies (para. 8).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Term</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Definition</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Question it answers</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Functional currency</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>the currency of the primary economic environment in which the entity operates</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>what the entity measures in</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Foreign currency</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>any currency other than the functional currency</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>which balances can produce exchange differences</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Presentation currency</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>the currency in which the statements are presented</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>what the reader sees</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The standard then works in two steps (paras. 17–18). Each entity, parent or subsidiary, measures its own transactions in its functional currency, and exchange differences on its foreign-currency items go to its profit or loss. Where an entity's functional currency differs from the group's presentation currency, its statements are then translated, and that difference goes to other comprehensive income (OCI). The second step is covered in the Other Comprehensive Income essay.</p><p>PHE's functional currency is the US dollar for every entity in the group except Elnusa, whose functional currency is the rupiah, and the consolidated statements are presented in dollars (Note 3a, Daftar 5/29, PDF p. 46; Note 3r(i), Daftar 5/66, PDF p. 83). For a PHE production-sharing subsidiary the rupiah is a foreign currency, even though it pays Indonesian taxes and Indonesian staff in it.</p><h2>2. Determining the functional currency</h2><p>The standard ranks the evidence for a functional currency, and management weighs it rather than picking a currency it prefers. The <em>primary indicators</em> look at operations (para. 9): the currency that mainly influences sales prices, often the currency prices are set and settled in; the currency of the country whose competitive forces and regulations mainly determine those prices; and the currency that mainly influences labour, material and other costs. <em>Secondary indicators</em> look at money (para. 10): the currency in which financing is raised and the currency in which operating receipts are kept. A foreign operation adds a third set (para. 11), centred on whether it is an extension of the parent or works with a significant degree of autonomy. When the indicators are mixed, management judges which currency most faithfully represents the economics, giving the primary indicators priority (para. 12).</p><p>For example (illustration), score three kinds of entity in PHE's group against the indicators. The reading of each indicator is this page's; PHE discloses no such analysis.</p><table style="min-width: 100px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Indicator</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Upstream PSC subsidiary</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Oilfield services company (Elnusa-type)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Overseas producer (M&amp;P-type)</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Sales prices</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>crude and gas priced in dollars</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>service fees mostly in rupiah</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>crude priced in dollars</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Costs</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>rigs, contractors and imported materials largely in dollars; staff and local services in rupiah</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>staff, fuel and local suppliers in rupiah</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>contractors and equipment in dollars</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Financing</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>dollar loans and sukuk</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>rupiah bank loans</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>dollar loans</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Result</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>US dollar</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>rupiah</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>US dollar</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>PHE's Note 1 lists Etablissements Maurel et Prom (M&amp;P), domiciled in Paris and 71.09 percent owned (Daftar 5/8, PDF p. 25), and Note 3r gives every entity but Elnusa the dollar as functional currency. Domicile and the currency of tax filings do not decide the matter.</p><p><strong>A change of functional currency</strong> happens only when the underlying transactions change (paras. 13 and 36), and it is accounted for prospectively (para. 35). Every item is translated at the rate on the date of change, and the translated amounts of non-monetary items become their historical cost (para. 37). For example (illustration), a services subsidiary wins long-term dollar contracts and switches from rupiah to dollars on 1 July, when the rate is Rp16,000 per dollar. A rig it carries at Rp80,000 million is then measured at:</p><blockquote><p>80,000 × 1,000 / 16,000 = 5,000, the rig's new historical cost in dollars</p></blockquote><p>Nothing is restated for earlier periods, and the rig's cost is never retranslated again.</p><h2>3. Initial recognition: the spot rate on the transaction date</h2><p>A foreign-currency transaction is one denominated in, or requiring settlement in, a foreign currency: buying or selling at a foreign-currency price, borrowing or lending in one, or acquiring assets or incurring liabilities in one (para. 20). It is recorded in the functional currency at the spot rate on the transaction date (para. 21), the date it first qualifies for recognition. An average rate for a week or a month may be used if rates do not fluctuate significantly (para. 22). PHE's policy restates the rule (Note 3r(ii)).</p><p>For example (illustration), on 15 November 2024 a PHE subsidiary receives an invoice from a local contractor for well services of Rp16,200 million, due in 90 days, when the rate is Rp15,800 per dollar.</p><blockquote><p>16,200 × 1,000 / 15,800 = 1,025.32</p><p>Dr Production expenses 1,025.32 / Cr Trade payables 1,025.32</p></blockquote><p><strong>Advance payments.</strong> When an entity pays or receives in advance, ISAK 122 <em>Foreign Currency Transactions and Advance Consideration</em> (formerly ISAK 33, IFRIC 22) fixes the transaction date as the date the prepayment or deferred income is first recognised. A prepayment is non-monetary (section 4), so the goods later received are measured at the rate of the advance. For example (illustration), the subsidiary prepays Rp1,580 million for chemicals when the rate is Rp15,800.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Advances for purchases 100.00 / Cr Cash 100.00</p><p>On delivery, at any later rate: Dr Inventories 100.00 / Cr Advances for purchases 100.00</p></blockquote><h2>4. At the reporting date: monetary and non-monetary items</h2><p>Monetary items follow the exchange rate after a transaction and non-monetary items carried at cost do not. The test is whether the item is a right to receive, or an obligation to deliver, a fixed or determinable number of units of currency (para. 16). Such <em>monetary items</em> are translated at the closing rate, the spot rate at the end of the period. <em>Non-monetary items</em> carried at historical cost keep the rate of the transaction date, and those carried at fair value use the rate when fair value was measured (para. 23).</p><table style="min-width: 50px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Monetary: retranslate at the closing rate</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Non-monetary at cost: keep the historical rate</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>cash and deposits</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>inventories</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>trade and other receivables</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>fixed assets, oil and gas properties</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>tax refunds receivable in cash, taxes payable</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>intangible assets, goodwill</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>trade payables, accrued expenses</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>right-of-use assets</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>bank loans, lease liabilities</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>prepayments and advances for goods or services</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>employee benefits and provisions to be paid in cash</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>provisions to be settled by delivering a non-monetary asset</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The logic is what each item will turn into. A rupiah receivable will produce a fixed number of rupiah, so its dollar value moves with the rate. A drilling rig bought in rupiah will produce oil, not rupiah, so its dollar cost stays where it was.</p><p>Take PHE's balances as the starting point. Note 48 shows rupiah tax refund claims (presented as other taxes receivable) of Rp7,551,277 million at the end of 2023, carried at 489,104, and rupiah taxes payable of Rp2,354,077 million, carried at 152,476 (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242). Both are monetary. PHE's policy names the Jakarta Interbank Spot Dollar Rate (JISDOR) as the closing rate (Note 3r(ii)); the amounts in Note 48 imply Rp15,439 per dollar at the end of 2023 and Rp16,157 at the end of 2024, which match the published JISDOR. Suppose, for illustration, both balances had stayed unchanged in rupiah through 2024.</p><blockquote><p>Tax refund claims: 7,551,277 × 1,000 / 16,157 = 467,369; loss = 489,104 − 467,369 = 21,735</p><p>Taxes payable: 2,354,077 × 1,000 / 16,157 = 145,700; gain = 152,476 − 145,700 = 6,776</p><p>Net loss = 21,735 − 6,776 = 14,959</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Dr Foreign exchange loss 21,735 / Cr Tax refund claims 21,735</p><p>Dr Other taxes payable 6,776 / Cr Foreign exchange gain 6,776</p></blockquote><p>The rupiah lost 4.44 percent of its dollar value (1 − 15,439 / 16,157), and each rupiah asset and liability changed by the same share.</p><p><strong>Items measured by comparing two amounts.</strong> For a non-monetary item tested against a recoverable figure, such as inventory at the lower of cost and net realisable value (NRV), each side is translated at the rate on the date it was determined (para. 25). For example (illustration), spares bought for Rp3,200 million at Rp15,500 have a year-end NRV of Rp3,250 million at Rp16,157.</p><blockquote><p>Cost = 3,200 × 1,000 / 15,500 = 206.45; NRV = 3,250 × 1,000 / 16,157 = 201.15</p><p>Write-down = 206.45 − 201.15 = 5.30</p></blockquote><p>The spares are worth more rupiah than they cost and still need a write-down in dollars (see the Inventories essay).</p><h2>5. Settlement, and where the differences go</h2><p>An exchange difference arises whenever a monetary item is settled, or retranslated, at a rate different from the one last used, and it goes to profit or loss in the period it arises (para. 28). If a transaction is settled in the same period, the whole difference falls there; if it spans a year end, each period takes the part caused by that period's rate change (para. 29). The total is fixed by the rate on the transaction date and the rate on settlement. The year end only splits it.</p><p>Return to section 3's invoice of Rp16,200 million booked at 1,025.32. At 31 December 2024 the closing rate is Rp16,157, and the subsidiary pays on 13 February 2025 at, for illustration, Rp16,300.</p><blockquote><p>31 December 2024: 16,200 × 1,000 / 16,157 = 1,002.66; gain = 1,025.32 − 1,002.66 = 22.66</p><p>13 February 2025: 16,200 × 1,000 / 16,300 = 993.87; gain = 1,002.66 − 993.87 = 8.80</p><p>Total gain = 1,025.32 − 993.87 = 31.45, the two steps before rounding (22.655 + 8.796)</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2025</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Entry</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade payables 22.66 / Cr Foreign exchange gain 22.66</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade payables 1,002.66 / Cr Cash 993.87, Cr Foreign exchange gain 8.80</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Kind</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>unrealised: the payable is retranslated</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>realised: rupiah are bought and paid</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The standard does not separate realised from unrealised differences; both are on the same line. The well services stay at 1,025.32 in production expense, because the expense was recognised once, at the transaction-date rate (para. 21).</p><p>Three exceptions send exchange differences elsewhere.</p><ul><li><p><em>Net investment in a foreign operation.</em> A long-term loan to a foreign operation whose settlement is neither planned nor likely is in substance part of the investment (para. 15). Its exchange differences go to profit or loss in the separate statements but to OCI in the consolidated ones, recycled on disposal (para. 32).</p></li><li><p><em>Hedging.</em> An exchange difference on a monetary item designated as a cash flow hedging instrument goes to OCI to the extent the hedge is effective (para. 27; PSAK 109). The Other Comprehensive Income essay teaches both of these routes.</p></li><li><p><em>Non-monetary items with gains in OCI.</em> When a gain on a non-monetary item goes to OCI, such as a revaluation surplus on a foreign-currency asset, its exchange component goes with it (para. 30).</p></li></ul><p><strong>Intragroup balances do not eliminate the exchange difference.</strong> Consolidation removes intragroup balances, but a monetary item between members with different functional currencies is a commitment to convert one currency into another, so its exchange difference stays in consolidated profit or loss (para. 45). For example (illustration), a dollar-functional PHE subsidiary is owed Rp15,439 million by Elnusa, rupiah-functional, from the end of 2023.</p><blockquote><p>Subsidiary: 15,439 × 1,000 / 15,439 = 1,000.00 at end 2023; 15,439 × 1,000 / 16,157 = 955.56 at end 2024; loss 44.44</p><p>Elnusa: owes Rp15,439 million in both years, no difference</p></blockquote><p>The receivable and payable still cancel on consolidation, but the 44.44 loss stays in the group's profit or loss.</p><h2>6. Dollar accounts, rupiah tax books</h2><p>The functional currency decides the accounting books, and the tax law decides the tax books, and in Indonesia the two can differ. The tax administration law requires books in rupiah and in Indonesian unless the Minister of Finance permits a foreign language and a currency other than rupiah (UU KUP art. 28(4) and (8)). Through 2024 the permission rule was PMK 196/PMK.03/2007, last amended by PMK 123/PMK.03/2019, which lists among the taxpayers that may keep books in English and US dollars production-sharing contractors (KKKS), mining contract-of-work holders, companies listed abroad, and taxpayers whose functional currency under Indonesian accounting standards is the dollar. PMK 81/2024 partly revoked it from 1 January 2025; permission now sits in its art. 455(3), and under PER-8/PJ/2025 art. 16 oil and gas contractors only notify, while dollar-functional taxpayers apply.</p><p>A company that keeps dollar accounts but rupiah tax books has two currencies inside one tax line. PSAK 212 <em>Income Taxes</em> (formerly PSAK 46, IAS 12) treats the effect of exchange rates on a tax base measured in another currency as a temporary difference, with the deferred tax charged to profit or loss (para. 41). For example (illustration), a dollar-functional subsidiary without dollar tax books owns a compressor bought for Rp154,390 million when the rate was Rp15,439, so its accounting cost is 10,000; ignore depreciation, and take the 22 percent Indonesian corporate rate.</p><blockquote><p>Tax base at end 2024 = 154,390 × 1,000 / 16,157 = 9,555.61</p><p>Taxable temporary difference = 10,000.00 − 9,555.61 = 444.39; deferred tax liability = 444.39 × 22% = 97.77</p><p>Dr Deferred tax expense 97.77 / Cr Deferred tax liability 97.77</p></blockquote><p>The rupiah's fall cut the future tax deductions measured in dollars, so tax expense rises with no change in the asset. A contractor allowed dollar tax books avoids this mismatch on its fixed assets (see the Deferred Tax essay).</p><h2>7. Presentation currency and foreign operations</h2><p>An entity may present its statements in any currency (para. 38). If that differs from its functional currency, it translates assets and liabilities at the closing rate, income and expenses at the rates on the transaction dates (an average rate is a common approximation, para. 40), and puts the resulting difference in OCI (para. 39). It must state the functional currency and the reason for presenting in another (para. 53). A dollar-functional company presenting in rupiah would carry a translation reserve though its business never changed.</p><p>The same procedure brings a foreign operation into consolidated statements. For PHE that means Elnusa, translated from rupiah into dollars with the difference in OCI and recycled when the operation is sold. The Other Comprehensive Income essay works it through with PHE's 2024 translation adjustments of −14,810.</p><h2>8. Hyperinflation and currencies that cannot be exchanged</h2><p>In a hyperinflationary economy historical amounts stop meaning anything, so translation alone would mislead. PSAK 229 <em>Financial Reporting in Hyperinflationary Economies</em> (formerly PSAK 63, IAS 29) gives no single threshold. Its indicators include people keeping wealth in a stable foreign currency, prices quoted in that currency, and cumulative inflation over three years approaching or exceeding 100 percent (para. 3). An entity whose functional currency is hyperinflationary restates its statements into the measuring unit current at the reporting date (para. 8). Monetary items are not restated, because they are already in current units (para. 12). Non-monetary items at cost are restated by a general price index from their acquisition date (paras. 14–15). Holding net monetary assets through inflation loses purchasing power, and the gain or loss on the net monetary position goes to profit or loss (paras. 27–28).</p><p>For example (illustration), an entity holds net monetary assets of 1,000 in local currency all year while the general price index rises from 120 to 180, and owns land bought for 2,000 when the index was 100.</p><blockquote><p>Loss on net monetary position = 1,000 × (180 / 120 − 1) = 500</p><p>Land restated = 2,000 × 180 / 100 = 3,600</p></blockquote><p>Only after restatement are the statements translated, all amounts at the latest closing rate (PSAK 221.42–43). An entity cannot avoid restatement by adopting its parent's currency (PSAK 221.14). PHE states that none of its subsidiaries has a hyperinflationary functional currency (Note 3r(iii), Daftar 5/67, PDF p. 84). Note 2d lists a 40 percent interest, held through the group, in Petroregional del Lago Mixed Company in Venezuela (Daftar 5/21, PDF p. 38), but the FS do not give that venture's functional currency.</p><p><strong>Lack of exchangeability.</strong> The 2023 amendments to IAS 21 require an entity to estimate a spot rate when a currency cannot be obtained through a normal exchange mechanism, and to disclose the effects (paras. 8A–8B, 19A, 57A). PHE lists the matching amendment to PSAK 221 as effective from 1 January 2025 and says it is assessing the impact (Note 3b, Daftar 5/31, PDF p. 48).</p><h2>9. Foreign exchange across industries</h2><p><strong>Coal and mineral exporters.</strong> Coal is priced in dollars, so a miner whose costs and debt are also in dollars finds the dollar functional under section 2, and it may keep dollar tax books under the same PMK list. Its exposure mirrors PHE's: rupiah VAT refunds, royalties and taxes payable, and local contractor payables. A miner with net rupiah liabilities gains when the rupiah weakens.</p><p><strong>Plantations.</strong> Palm oil is priced against dollar and ringgit benchmarks while estate costs are in rupiah, so para. 12's judgement decides.</p><p><strong>Airlines.</strong> Aircraft leases, fuel and maintenance are priced in dollars, and Garuda Indonesia keeps its financial statements in dollars, so for it the rupiah is the foreign currency. For a rupiah-functional airline the mismatch is built into the lease accounting, because the lease liability is monetary and the right-of-use asset is not (PSAK 116; see the Right-of-Use Assets essay). For example (illustration), a rupiah airline leases an aircraft with a liability of USD 100 million at Rp15,439 at the end of 2023; ignore payments and interest.</p><blockquote><p>Liability at end 2024 = 100 × 16,157 = Rp1,615,700 million; at end 2023 = 100 × 15,439 = Rp1,543,900 million</p><p>Exchange loss = Rp71,800 million; the right-of-use asset stays at Rp1,543,900 million less depreciation</p></blockquote><p><strong>Banks.</strong> A rupiah-functional bank revalues its foreign-currency positions daily, and the differences are trading results. Bank Indonesia caps the net open position, assets less liabilities in foreign currency including off-balance-sheet positions, at 20 percent of capital (PBI 5/13/PBI/2003 as last amended by PBI 17/5/PBI/2015). For example (illustration), a bank with capital of Rp10,000,000 million is long USD 120 million.</p><blockquote><p>Position at end 2023 = 120 × 15,439 = Rp1,852,680 million, 18.5% of capital</p><p>At end 2024 = 120 × 16,157 = Rp1,938,840 million, 19.4% of capital; revaluation gain Rp86,160 million</p></blockquote><p>The weakening that gives the bank a gain also pushes it toward the limit without a single new trade.</p><h2>10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Notes 48 and 49</h2><p>PHE's 2024 loss is consistent with a net rupiah monetary asset held while the rupiah weakened from Rp15,439 to Rp16,157. PSAK 221 requires the amount of exchange differences recognised in profit or loss (para. 52(a)), which PHE shows on the face of the statement, and Note 48 lists the monetary items behind it (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Rupiah items, Rp million</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash and cash equivalents</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>7,160,012</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>16,313,774</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Restricted cash</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>236,922</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>866,668</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Trade and other receivables</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,935,915</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,029,055</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Tax refund claims</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>9,103,678</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>7,551,277</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Trade and other payables</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(9,328,584)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(6,712,954)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Shareholder loan, bank loans</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(740,182)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(799,484)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Taxes payable</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(2,473,124)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(2,354,077)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Accrued expenses and other provisions</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(6,611,848)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(4,486,126)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Net rupiah monetary assets/(liabilities)</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>(717,207)</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>12,408,133</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>At the JISDOR closing rate, USD thousand</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(44,390)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>803,688</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The 2024 rows sum to Rp717,211 million against the printed Rp717,207 million (rounding). Note 48's own bottom line, which adds euro and other currencies, is a net asset of 8,371 (2023: 889,478).</p><p>The implied rates match JISDOR at both dates (9,103,678 / 563,451 = Rp16,157; 7,551,277 / 489,104 = Rp15,439), and the tax refund claims equal Note 43a's total other taxes receivable. Rupiah cash ties to Note 6 (78 on hand, 375,921 in banks and 67,154 in deposits, total 443,153; Daftar 5/90–5/92, PDF pp. 107–109).</p><p>Had the opening net asset of 803,688 been held all year, the loss would have been:</p><blockquote><p>12,408,133 × 1,000 / 16,157 = 767,973; loss = 803,688 − 767,973 = 35,715</p></blockquote><p>The reported loss is 4,479. The position fell by 848,078 during the year (803,688 + 44,390), led by rupiah cash, which fell from 1,056,660 to 443,152 at the Note 48 rates as rupiah were spent. The rupiah's path within the year also matters, because differences are realised at settlement rates. The year-end position is a weak guide even to direction: in 2023 PHE held net rupiah assets while the rupiah strengthened to Rp15,439, and still reported a loss of 19,772. The cash flow statement's effect of exchange rate changes on cash, −19,739 (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17), is no comparator, because it also carries the translation of Elnusa's rupiah cash, which goes to OCI.</p><p>The segment note splits the loss into −6,466 from exploration and production, +29 from services and +1,958 of elimination (Note 46, Daftar 5/220, PDF p. 237). The elimination is consistent with intragroup balances between Elnusa and dollar-functional members (section 5); PHE does not explain it.</p><p>Note 49 describes PHE's exposure as minimal, because most revenue and operating spending are in dollars (Daftar 5/227, PDF p. 244), and gives a sensitivity: a 5 percent stronger rupiah would raise profit by 19,202 and a 5 percent weaker one lower it by 17,373 at the end of 2024 (Daftar 5/228, PDF p. 245). The asymmetry fits a net rupiah asset.</p><blockquote><p>Implied net rupiah asset, USD thousand = 19,202 / (1 / 0.95 − 1) = 364,838; check: 17,373 / (1 − 1 / 1.05) = 364,833</p></blockquote><p>That is Note 48's net rupiah position without the accrued expenses and other provisions line: (−717,207 + 6,611,848) × 1,000 / 16,157 = 364,835, and for 2023 1,094,259 against 1,094,267. Note 49's sensitivity leaves that line out; PHE does not say why.</p><h2>11. Upstream note: why a PSC contractor is a dollar company</h2><p>PHE's PSC subsidiaries sell crude and gas at dollar prices, recover costs in dollars, and borrow in dollars, which makes the dollar their functional currency under section 2. Their rupiah exposure comes from the Indonesian state and the local supply chain: reimbursable VAT claims, taxes payable, rupiah accruals and payables to local contractors, and employee benefits paid in rupiah. Note 25 shows foreign exchange changes of −9,019 on the obligation and −6,802 on plan assets for 2024, a net −2,217 (Daftar 5/122, PDF p. 139), although Note 48 lists no employee-benefit liability. Section 6's tax mismatch is avoided where the contract and the PMK allow dollar books, which the FS do not discuss.</p><h2>12. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/other-comprehensive-income">other-comprehensive-income</a> and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/other-comprehensive-income">oci-translation-adjustments</a>: translation of Elnusa, net investment and hedges.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/other-taxes-receivable-current">other-taxes-receivable-current</a>, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/other-taxes-receivable-current">other-taxes-receivable-non-current</a>, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/other-taxes-receivable-current">other-taxes-payable</a>: the largest rupiah monetary items.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/cash-and-cash-equivalents">cash-and-cash-equivalents</a>: rupiah cash and the cash-flow effect of exchange rates.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/trade-payables">trade-payables</a>, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/accrued-expenses">accrued-expenses</a>: rupiah liabilities that offset the assets.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/advances-prepayments-current">advances-prepayments-current</a>: non-monetary prepayments under ISAK 122.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets">deferred-tax-liabilities</a>: temporary differences from rupiah tax bases.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>Note 49's sensitivity excludes Note 48's accrued expenses and other provisions (it reconciles within 9 in both years) but keeps the tax refund claims and taxes payable, which are not contractual financial instruments either. The basis for the exclusion is not disclosed.</p></li><li><p>Note 48 omits employee benefits; the Note 25 exchange rows may be partly Elnusa's translation. Note 3r also prints the MYR rate as USD 0.27 for 2024 against about 0.22 in the market (not verified).</p></li><li><p>The reconciliation of the 35,715 static loss to the reported 4,479 is a reading. PHE does not split the line into realised and unrealised parts or by currency, and the 2023 loss runs against the year-end position.</p></li><li><p>The indicator scoring in section 2 is an illustration of how the indicators would read. PHE does not disclose how it determined each entity's functional currency, nor the functional currency of the Venezuelan venture.</p></li><li><p>That PHE's PSC subsidiaries keep dollar tax books is inferred from the KKKS entry in PMK 196/2007 as amended. The FS are silent on tax bookkeeping currency.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of profit or loss and OCI (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 1 (Daftar 5/8, PDF p. 25); Note 2d (Daftar 5/21, PDF p. 38); Note 3a (Daftar 5/29, PDF p. 46); Note 3b, amendment to PSAK 221 (Daftar 5/31, PDF p. 48); Note 3r (Daftar 5/66–5/67, PDF pp. 83–84); Note 6 (Daftar 5/90–5/92, PDF pp. 107–109); Note 25 (Daftar 5/122, PDF p. 139); Note 46 (Daftar 5/220, PDF p. 237); Note 48 (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242); Note 49b(i) (Daftar 5/227–5/228, PDF pp. 244–245). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk; Indonesian and English columns read; line figure checked against the PHE FS 2024 data extract).</p></li><li><p>The order follows PSAK 221's own structure. Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting</em>, IFRS edition, is background only. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 221 (IAS 21), paras. 8–8B, 9–17, 19A, 20–30, 32, 34–43, 45, 52–53 and the lack-of-exchangeability effective date (1 January 2025): VERIFIED (web, AASB 121 compiled text, standards.aasb.gov.au, which reproduces IAS 21). Indonesian effective date of the amendment: as stated in PHE Note 3b.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 229 (IAS 29), paras. 3, 8, 11–15, 27–28: VERIFIED (web, AASB 129, standards.aasb.gov.au).</p></li><li><p>New numbers PSAK 221, PSAK 229, ISAK 122, PSAK 207, PSAK 212, PSAK 116: VERIFIED (web, IAI, "Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia", effective 1 January 2024). Old numbers (PSAK 10, 63, 46; ISAK 33) from the renumbering rule; content of ISAK 122 and PSAK 212.41: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>JISDOR Rp16,157 on 31 December 2024: VERIFIED (web, Bank Indonesia post on X, 31 Dec 2024; also implied by Note 48 to four decimals). Rp16,162, quoted elsewhere for that date, is BI's middle rate, not JISDOR. Rp15,439 at the end of 2023: VERIFIED (web, secondary: Kontan, 31 July 2024). Both match the rates implied by PHE Note 48.</p></li><li><p>UU KUP art. 28(4) and (8): VERIFIED (web, secondary: pasal.id; Ortax). PMK 196/PMK.03/2007 as amended by PMK 24/PMK.011/2012, PMK 1/PMK.03/2015 and PMK 123/PMK.03/2019, taxpayer list including KKKS and dollar-functional taxpayers: VERIFIED (web, secondary: DDTC glossary citing PMK 123/2019 art. 3(1)); partial revocation by PMK 81/2024 from 1 January 2025: VERIFIED (web, JDIH Kemenkeu status page). Successor rules PMK 81/2024 art. 455(3) and PER-8/PJ/2025 art. 16(4)–(5): VERIFIED (web, secondary: Ortax, 15 July 2025). Which articles of PMK 196 were revoked: NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Net open position cap of 20 percent of capital, PBI 5/13/PBI/2003 as last amended by PBI 17/5/PBI/2015: VERIFIED (web, bi.go.id; OJK). No revoking regulation found. Garuda Indonesia reporting in dollars since 2012: VERIFIED (web, secondary: Kontan; Indonesia Investments). The Indonesian corporate tax rate of 22 percent is as used across this series.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Every entity first determines its functional currency, the currency of the economic environment that mainly sets its prices and costs, from evidence the standard ranks. Any other currency is foreign. A Jakarta company can be a dollar company, and a Paris one can be too.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Foreign-currency transactions are recorded at the spot rate on the transaction date. At each reporting date, monetary items (cash, receivables, payables, provisions to be paid in cash) are retranslated at the closing rate and the difference goes to profit or loss. Non-monetary items carried at cost (inventory, fixed assets, prepayments) keep their historical rate.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An exchange loss therefore measures the net monetary position held in a foreign currency and how the rate moved while it was held. A dollar-functional company that holds more rupiah assets than rupiah liabilities loses when the rupiah weakens. Translation of a foreign operation into a presentation currency is a different step that goes to other comprehensive income, and hyperinflation (PSAK 229) adds a restatement before any translation.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report a foreign exchange loss, net, of 4,479 for 2024 against 19,772 for 2023, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of profit or loss and other comprehensive income, Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The loss is 0.09 percent of profit before income tax of 4,892,576 (0.44 percent of 4,492,029 in 2023). The line has no note of its own.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After reading it a student should be able to determine an entity's functional currency, record a foreign-currency transaction from invoice to settlement, sort balances into monetary and non-monetary, and explain why a dollar company reports a gain or loss when the rupiah moves.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated; rupiah amounts are in millions. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. Three currencies, three questions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A group that buys in rupiah, sells in dollars and reports to a parent abroad needs a rule for which currency its books measure in. PSAK 221 ","type":"text"},{"text":"The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 10, IAS 21) separates three currencies (para. 8).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Term","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Definition","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Question it answers","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Functional currency","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"the currency of the primary economic environment in which the entity operates","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"what the entity measures in","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Foreign currency","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"any currency other than the functional currency","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"which balances can produce exchange differences","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Presentation currency","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"the currency in which the statements are presented","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"what the reader sees","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The standard then works in two steps (paras. 17–18). Each entity, parent or subsidiary, measures its own transactions in its functional currency, and exchange differences on its foreign-currency items go to its profit or loss. Where an entity's functional currency differs from the group's presentation currency, its statements are then translated, and that difference goes to other comprehensive income (OCI). The second step is covered in the Other Comprehensive Income essay.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's functional currency is the US dollar for every entity in the group except Elnusa, whose functional currency is the rupiah, and the consolidated statements are presented in dollars (Note 3a, Daftar 5/29, PDF p. 46; Note 3r(i), Daftar 5/66, PDF p. 83). For a PHE production-sharing subsidiary the rupiah is a foreign currency, even though it pays Indonesian taxes and Indonesian staff in it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Determining the functional currency","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The standard ranks the evidence for a functional currency, and management weighs it rather than picking a currency it prefers. The ","type":"text"},{"text":"primary indicators","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" look at operations (para. 9): the currency that mainly influences sales prices, often the currency prices are set and settled in; the currency of the country whose competitive forces and regulations mainly determine those prices; and the currency that mainly influences labour, material and other costs. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Secondary indicators","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" look at money (para. 10): the currency in which financing is raised and the currency in which operating receipts are kept. A foreign operation adds a third set (para. 11), centred on whether it is an extension of the parent or works with a significant degree of autonomy. When the indicators are mixed, management judges which currency most faithfully represents the economics, giving the primary indicators priority (para. 12).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), score three kinds of entity in PHE's group against the indicators. The reading of each indicator is this page's; PHE discloses no such analysis.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Indicator","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Upstream PSC subsidiary","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Oilfield services company (Elnusa-type)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Overseas producer (M&P-type)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Sales prices","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"crude and gas priced in dollars","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"service fees mostly in rupiah","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"crude priced in dollars","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Costs","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"rigs, contractors and imported materials largely in dollars; staff and local services in rupiah","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"staff, fuel and local suppliers in rupiah","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"contractors and equipment in dollars","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Financing","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"dollar loans and sukuk","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"rupiah bank loans","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"dollar loans","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Result","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"US dollar","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"rupiah","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"US dollar","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's Note 1 lists Etablissements Maurel et Prom (M&P), domiciled in Paris and 71.09 percent owned (Daftar 5/8, PDF p. 25), and Note 3r gives every entity but Elnusa the dollar as functional currency. Domicile and the currency of tax filings do not decide the matter.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A change of functional currency","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" happens only when the underlying transactions change (paras. 13 and 36), and it is accounted for prospectively (para. 35). Every item is translated at the rate on the date of change, and the translated amounts of non-monetary items become their historical cost (para. 37). For example (illustration), a services subsidiary wins long-term dollar contracts and switches from rupiah to dollars on 1 July, when the rate is Rp16,000 per dollar. A rig it carries at Rp80,000 million is then measured at:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"80,000 × 1,000 / 16,000 = 5,000, the rig's new historical cost in dollars","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Nothing is restated for earlier periods, and the rig's cost is never retranslated again.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Initial recognition: the spot rate on the transaction date","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A foreign-currency transaction is one denominated in, or requiring settlement in, a foreign currency: buying or selling at a foreign-currency price, borrowing or lending in one, or acquiring assets or incurring liabilities in one (para. 20). It is recorded in the functional currency at the spot rate on the transaction date (para. 21), the date it first qualifies for recognition. An average rate for a week or a month may be used if rates do not fluctuate significantly (para. 22). PHE's policy restates the rule (Note 3r(ii)).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), on 15 November 2024 a PHE subsidiary receives an invoice from a local contractor for well services of Rp16,200 million, due in 90 days, when the rate is Rp15,800 per dollar.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"16,200 × 1,000 / 15,800 = 1,025.32","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Production expenses 1,025.32 / Cr Trade payables 1,025.32","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Advance payments.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" When an entity pays or receives in advance, ISAK 122 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Foreign Currency Transactions and Advance Consideration","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly ISAK 33, IFRIC 22) fixes the transaction date as the date the prepayment or deferred income is first recognised. A prepayment is non-monetary (section 4), so the goods later received are measured at the rate of the advance. For example (illustration), the subsidiary prepays Rp1,580 million for chemicals when the rate is Rp15,800.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Advances for purchases 100.00 / Cr Cash 100.00","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"On delivery, at any later rate: Dr Inventories 100.00 / Cr Advances for purchases 100.00","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. At the reporting date: monetary and non-monetary items","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Monetary items follow the exchange rate after a transaction and non-monetary items carried at cost do not. The test is whether the item is a right to receive, or an obligation to deliver, a fixed or determinable number of units of currency (para. 16). Such ","type":"text"},{"text":"monetary items","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" are translated at the closing rate, the spot rate at the end of the period. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Non-monetary items","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" carried at historical cost keep the rate of the transaction date, and those carried at fair value use the rate when fair value was measured (para. 23).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Monetary: retranslate at the closing rate","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Non-monetary at cost: keep the historical rate","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"cash and deposits","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"inventories","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"trade and other receivables","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"fixed assets, oil and gas properties","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"tax refunds receivable in cash, taxes payable","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"intangible assets, goodwill","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"trade payables, accrued expenses","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"right-of-use assets","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"bank loans, lease liabilities","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"prepayments and advances for goods or services","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"employee benefits and provisions to be paid in cash","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"provisions to be settled by delivering a non-monetary asset","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The logic is what each item will turn into. A rupiah receivable will produce a fixed number of rupiah, so its dollar value moves with the rate. A drilling rig bought in rupiah will produce oil, not rupiah, so its dollar cost stays where it was.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Take PHE's balances as the starting point. Note 48 shows rupiah tax refund claims (presented as other taxes receivable) of Rp7,551,277 million at the end of 2023, carried at 489,104, and rupiah taxes payable of Rp2,354,077 million, carried at 152,476 (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242). Both are monetary. PHE's policy names the Jakarta Interbank Spot Dollar Rate (JISDOR) as the closing rate (Note 3r(ii)); the amounts in Note 48 imply Rp15,439 per dollar at the end of 2023 and Rp16,157 at the end of 2024, which match the published JISDOR. Suppose, for illustration, both balances had stayed unchanged in rupiah through 2024.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Tax refund claims: 7,551,277 × 1,000 / 16,157 = 467,369; loss = 489,104 − 467,369 = 21,735","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Taxes payable: 2,354,077 × 1,000 / 16,157 = 145,700; gain = 152,476 − 145,700 = 6,776","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net loss = 21,735 − 6,776 = 14,959","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Foreign exchange loss 21,735 / Cr Tax refund claims 21,735","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Other taxes payable 6,776 / Cr Foreign exchange gain 6,776","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The rupiah lost 4.44 percent of its dollar value (1 − 15,439 / 16,157), and each rupiah asset and liability changed by the same share.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Items measured by comparing two amounts.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" For a non-monetary item tested against a recoverable figure, such as inventory at the lower of cost and net realisable value (NRV), each side is translated at the rate on the date it was determined (para. 25). For example (illustration), spares bought for Rp3,200 million at Rp15,500 have a year-end NRV of Rp3,250 million at Rp16,157.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost = 3,200 × 1,000 / 15,500 = 206.45; NRV = 3,250 × 1,000 / 16,157 = 201.15","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Write-down = 206.45 − 201.15 = 5.30","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The spares are worth more rupiah than they cost and still need a write-down in dollars (see the Inventories essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Settlement, and where the differences go","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An exchange difference arises whenever a monetary item is settled, or retranslated, at a rate different from the one last used, and it goes to profit or loss in the period it arises (para. 28). If a transaction is settled in the same period, the whole difference falls there; if it spans a year end, each period takes the part caused by that period's rate change (para. 29). The total is fixed by the rate on the transaction date and the rate on settlement. The year end only splits it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Return to section 3's invoice of Rp16,200 million booked at 1,025.32. At 31 December 2024 the closing rate is Rp16,157, and the subsidiary pays on 13 February 2025 at, for illustration, Rp16,300.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 December 2024: 16,200 × 1,000 / 16,157 = 1,002.66; gain = 1,025.32 − 1,002.66 = 22.66","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"13 February 2025: 16,200 × 1,000 / 16,300 = 993.87; gain = 1,002.66 − 993.87 = 8.80","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total gain = 1,025.32 − 993.87 = 31.45, the two steps before rounding (22.655 + 8.796)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Entry","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 22.66 / Cr Foreign exchange gain 22.66","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 1,002.66 / Cr Cash 993.87, Cr Foreign exchange gain 8.80","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kind","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"unrealised: the payable is retranslated","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"realised: rupiah are bought and paid","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The standard does not separate realised from unrealised differences; both are on the same line. The well services stay at 1,025.32 in production expense, because the expense was recognised once, at the transaction-date rate (para. 21).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Three exceptions send exchange differences elsewhere.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net investment in a foreign operation.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" A long-term loan to a foreign operation whose settlement is neither planned nor likely is in substance part of the investment (para. 15). Its exchange differences go to profit or loss in the separate statements but to OCI in the consolidated ones, recycled on disposal (para. 32).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Hedging.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" An exchange difference on a monetary item designated as a cash flow hedging instrument goes to OCI to the extent the hedge is effective (para. 27; PSAK 109). The Other Comprehensive Income essay teaches both of these routes.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Non-monetary items with gains in OCI.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" When a gain on a non-monetary item goes to OCI, such as a revaluation surplus on a foreign-currency asset, its exchange component goes with it (para. 30).","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Intragroup balances do not eliminate the exchange difference.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Consolidation removes intragroup balances, but a monetary item between members with different functional currencies is a commitment to convert one currency into another, so its exchange difference stays in consolidated profit or loss (para. 45). For example (illustration), a dollar-functional PHE subsidiary is owed Rp15,439 million by Elnusa, rupiah-functional, from the end of 2023.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Subsidiary: 15,439 × 1,000 / 15,439 = 1,000.00 at end 2023; 15,439 × 1,000 / 16,157 = 955.56 at end 2024; loss 44.44","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Elnusa: owes Rp15,439 million in both years, no difference","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The receivable and payable still cancel on consolidation, but the 44.44 loss stays in the group's profit or loss.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Dollar accounts, rupiah tax books","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The functional currency decides the accounting books, and the tax law decides the tax books, and in Indonesia the two can differ. The tax administration law requires books in rupiah and in Indonesian unless the Minister of Finance permits a foreign language and a currency other than rupiah (UU KUP art. 28(4) and (8)). Through 2024 the permission rule was PMK 196/PMK.03/2007, last amended by PMK 123/PMK.03/2019, which lists among the taxpayers that may keep books in English and US dollars production-sharing contractors (KKKS), mining contract-of-work holders, companies listed abroad, and taxpayers whose functional currency under Indonesian accounting standards is the dollar. PMK 81/2024 partly revoked it from 1 January 2025; permission now sits in its art. 455(3), and under PER-8/PJ/2025 art. 16 oil and gas contractors only notify, while dollar-functional taxpayers apply.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A company that keeps dollar accounts but rupiah tax books has two currencies inside one tax line. PSAK 212 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Income Taxes","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 46, IAS 12) treats the effect of exchange rates on a tax base measured in another currency as a temporary difference, with the deferred tax charged to profit or loss (para. 41). For example (illustration), a dollar-functional subsidiary without dollar tax books owns a compressor bought for Rp154,390 million when the rate was Rp15,439, so its accounting cost is 10,000; ignore depreciation, and take the 22 percent Indonesian corporate rate.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Tax base at end 2024 = 154,390 × 1,000 / 16,157 = 9,555.61","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Taxable temporary difference = 10,000.00 − 9,555.61 = 444.39; deferred tax liability = 444.39 × 22% = 97.77","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Deferred tax expense 97.77 / Cr Deferred tax liability 97.77","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The rupiah's fall cut the future tax deductions measured in dollars, so tax expense rises with no change in the asset. A contractor allowed dollar tax books avoids this mismatch on its fixed assets (see the Deferred Tax essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Presentation currency and foreign operations","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An entity may present its statements in any currency (para. 38). If that differs from its functional currency, it translates assets and liabilities at the closing rate, income and expenses at the rates on the transaction dates (an average rate is a common approximation, para. 40), and puts the resulting difference in OCI (para. 39). It must state the functional currency and the reason for presenting in another (para. 53). A dollar-functional company presenting in rupiah would carry a translation reserve though its business never changed.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The same procedure brings a foreign operation into consolidated statements. For PHE that means Elnusa, translated from rupiah into dollars with the difference in OCI and recycled when the operation is sold. The Other Comprehensive Income essay works it through with PHE's 2024 translation adjustments of −14,810.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Hyperinflation and currencies that cannot be exchanged","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In a hyperinflationary economy historical amounts stop meaning anything, so translation alone would mislead. PSAK 229 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Financial Reporting in Hyperinflationary Economies","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 63, IAS 29) gives no single threshold. Its indicators include people keeping wealth in a stable foreign currency, prices quoted in that currency, and cumulative inflation over three years approaching or exceeding 100 percent (para. 3). An entity whose functional currency is hyperinflationary restates its statements into the measuring unit current at the reporting date (para. 8). Monetary items are not restated, because they are already in current units (para. 12). Non-monetary items at cost are restated by a general price index from their acquisition date (paras. 14–15). Holding net monetary assets through inflation loses purchasing power, and the gain or loss on the net monetary position goes to profit or loss (paras. 27–28).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), an entity holds net monetary assets of 1,000 in local currency all year while the general price index rises from 120 to 180, and owns land bought for 2,000 when the index was 100.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Loss on net monetary position = 1,000 × (180 / 120 − 1) = 500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Land restated = 2,000 × 180 / 100 = 3,600","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Only after restatement are the statements translated, all amounts at the latest closing rate (PSAK 221.42–43). An entity cannot avoid restatement by adopting its parent's currency (PSAK 221.14). PHE states that none of its subsidiaries has a hyperinflationary functional currency (Note 3r(iii), Daftar 5/67, PDF p. 84). Note 2d lists a 40 percent interest, held through the group, in Petroregional del Lago Mixed Company in Venezuela (Daftar 5/21, PDF p. 38), but the FS do not give that venture's functional currency.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lack of exchangeability.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The 2023 amendments to IAS 21 require an entity to estimate a spot rate when a currency cannot be obtained through a normal exchange mechanism, and to disclose the effects (paras. 8A–8B, 19A, 57A). PHE lists the matching amendment to PSAK 221 as effective from 1 January 2025 and says it is assessing the impact (Note 3b, Daftar 5/31, PDF p. 48).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Foreign exchange across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Coal and mineral exporters.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Coal is priced in dollars, so a miner whose costs and debt are also in dollars finds the dollar functional under section 2, and it may keep dollar tax books under the same PMK list. Its exposure mirrors PHE's: rupiah VAT refunds, royalties and taxes payable, and local contractor payables. A miner with net rupiah liabilities gains when the rupiah weakens.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Plantations.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Palm oil is priced against dollar and ringgit benchmarks while estate costs are in rupiah, so para. 12's judgement decides.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Airlines.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Aircraft leases, fuel and maintenance are priced in dollars, and Garuda Indonesia keeps its financial statements in dollars, so for it the rupiah is the foreign currency. For a rupiah-functional airline the mismatch is built into the lease accounting, because the lease liability is monetary and the right-of-use asset is not (PSAK 116; see the Right-of-Use Assets essay). For example (illustration), a rupiah airline leases an aircraft with a liability of USD 100 million at Rp15,439 at the end of 2023; ignore payments and interest.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Liability at end 2024 = 100 × 16,157 = Rp1,615,700 million; at end 2023 = 100 × 15,439 = Rp1,543,900 million","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Exchange loss = Rp71,800 million; the right-of-use asset stays at Rp1,543,900 million less depreciation","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Banks.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A rupiah-functional bank revalues its foreign-currency positions daily, and the differences are trading results. Bank Indonesia caps the net open position, assets less liabilities in foreign currency including off-balance-sheet positions, at 20 percent of capital (PBI 5/13/PBI/2003 as last amended by PBI 17/5/PBI/2015). For example (illustration), a bank with capital of Rp10,000,000 million is long USD 120 million.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Position at end 2023 = 120 × 15,439 = Rp1,852,680 million, 18.5% of capital","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"At end 2024 = 120 × 16,157 = Rp1,938,840 million, 19.4% of capital; revaluation gain Rp86,160 million","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The weakening that gives the bank a gain also pushes it toward the limit without a single new trade.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Notes 48 and 49","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's 2024 loss is consistent with a net rupiah monetary asset held while the rupiah weakened from Rp15,439 to Rp16,157. PSAK 221 requires the amount of exchange differences recognised in profit or loss (para. 52(a)), which PHE shows on the face of the statement, and Note 48 lists the monetary items behind it (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Rupiah items, Rp million","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2023","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash and cash equivalents","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7,160,012","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"16,313,774","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Restricted cash","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"236,922","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"866,668","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade and other receivables","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,935,915","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,029,055","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Tax refund claims","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9,103,678","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7,551,277","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade and other payables","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(9,328,584)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(6,712,954)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Shareholder loan, bank loans","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(740,182)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(799,484)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Taxes payable","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(2,473,124)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(2,354,077)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accrued expenses and other provisions","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(6,611,848)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(4,486,126)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net rupiah monetary assets/(liabilities)","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(717,207)","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12,408,133","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"At the JISDOR closing rate, USD thousand","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(44,390)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"803,688","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 2024 rows sum to Rp717,211 million against the printed Rp717,207 million (rounding). Note 48's own bottom line, which adds euro and other currencies, is a net asset of 8,371 (2023: 889,478).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The implied rates match JISDOR at both dates (9,103,678 / 563,451 = Rp16,157; 7,551,277 / 489,104 = Rp15,439), and the tax refund claims equal Note 43a's total other taxes receivable. Rupiah cash ties to Note 6 (78 on hand, 375,921 in banks and 67,154 in deposits, total 443,153; Daftar 5/90–5/92, PDF pp. 107–109).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Had the opening net asset of 803,688 been held all year, the loss would have been:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12,408,133 × 1,000 / 16,157 = 767,973; loss = 803,688 − 767,973 = 35,715","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The reported loss is 4,479. The position fell by 848,078 during the year (803,688 + 44,390), led by rupiah cash, which fell from 1,056,660 to 443,152 at the Note 48 rates as rupiah were spent. The rupiah's path within the year also matters, because differences are realised at settlement rates. The year-end position is a weak guide even to direction: in 2023 PHE held net rupiah assets while the rupiah strengthened to Rp15,439, and still reported a loss of 19,772. The cash flow statement's effect of exchange rate changes on cash, −19,739 (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17), is no comparator, because it also carries the translation of Elnusa's rupiah cash, which goes to OCI.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The segment note splits the loss into −6,466 from exploration and production, +29 from services and +1,958 of elimination (Note 46, Daftar 5/220, PDF p. 237). The elimination is consistent with intragroup balances between Elnusa and dollar-functional members (section 5); PHE does not explain it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 49 describes PHE's exposure as minimal, because most revenue and operating spending are in dollars (Daftar 5/227, PDF p. 244), and gives a sensitivity: a 5 percent stronger rupiah would raise profit by 19,202 and a 5 percent weaker one lower it by 17,373 at the end of 2024 (Daftar 5/228, PDF p. 245). The asymmetry fits a net rupiah asset.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Implied net rupiah asset, USD thousand = 19,202 / (1 / 0.95 − 1) = 364,838; check: 17,373 / (1 − 1 / 1.05) = 364,833","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That is Note 48's net rupiah position without the accrued expenses and other provisions line: (−717,207 + 6,611,848) × 1,000 / 16,157 = 364,835, and for 2023 1,094,259 against 1,094,267. Note 49's sensitivity leaves that line out; PHE does not say why.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11. Upstream note: why a PSC contractor is a dollar company","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's PSC subsidiaries sell crude and gas at dollar prices, recover costs in dollars, and borrow in dollars, which makes the dollar their functional currency under section 2. Their rupiah exposure comes from the Indonesian state and the local supply chain: reimbursable VAT claims, taxes payable, rupiah accruals and payables to local contractors, and employee benefits paid in rupiah. Note 25 shows foreign exchange changes of −9,019 on the obligation and −6,802 on plan assets for 2024, a net −2,217 (Daftar 5/122, PDF p. 139), although Note 48 lists no employee-benefit liability. Section 6's tax mismatch is avoided where the contract and the PMK allow dollar books, which the FS do not discuss.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other-comprehensive-income","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/other-comprehensive-income","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"text":"oci-translation-adjustments","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/other-comprehensive-income","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": translation of Elnusa, net investment and hedges.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other-taxes-receivable-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/other-taxes-receivable-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"text":"other-taxes-receivable-non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/other-taxes-receivable-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"text":"other-taxes-payable","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/other-taxes-receivable-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the largest rupiah monetary items.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"cash-and-cash-equivalents","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/cash-and-cash-equivalents","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": rupiah cash and the cash-flow effect of exchange rates.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"trade-payables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/trade-payables","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"text":"accrued-expenses","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/accrued-expenses","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": rupiah liabilities that offset the assets.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"advances-prepayments-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/advances-prepayments-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": non-monetary prepayments under ISAK 122.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"deferred-tax-liabilities","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": temporary differences from rupiah tax bases.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 49's sensitivity excludes Note 48's accrued expenses and other provisions (it reconciles within 9 in both years) but keeps the tax refund claims and taxes payable, which are not contractual financial instruments either. The basis for the exclusion is not disclosed.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 48 omits employee benefits; the Note 25 exchange rows may be partly Elnusa's translation. Note 3r also prints the MYR rate as USD 0.27 for 2024 against about 0.22 in the market (not verified).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The reconciliation of the 35,715 static loss to the reported 4,479 is a reading. PHE does not split the line into realised and unrealised parts or by currency, and the 2023 loss runs against the year-end position.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The indicator scoring in section 2 is an illustration of how the indicators would read. PHE does not disclose how it determined each entity's functional currency, nor the functional currency of the Venezuelan venture.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That PHE's PSC subsidiaries keep dollar tax books is inferred from the KKKS entry in PMK 196/2007 as amended. The FS are silent on tax bookkeeping currency.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of profit or loss and OCI (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 1 (Daftar 5/8, PDF p. 25); Note 2d (Daftar 5/21, PDF p. 38); Note 3a (Daftar 5/29, PDF p. 46); Note 3b, amendment to PSAK 221 (Daftar 5/31, PDF p. 48); Note 3r (Daftar 5/66–5/67, PDF pp. 83–84); Note 6 (Daftar 5/90–5/92, PDF pp. 107–109); Note 25 (Daftar 5/122, PDF p. 139); Note 46 (Daftar 5/220, PDF p. 237); Note 48 (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242); Note 49b(i) (Daftar 5/227–5/228, PDF pp. 244–245). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk; Indonesian and English columns read; line figure checked against the PHE FS 2024 data extract).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The order follows PSAK 221's own structure. Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", IFRS edition, is background only. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 221 (IAS 21), paras. 8–8B, 9–17, 19A, 20–30, 32, 34–43, 45, 52–53 and the lack-of-exchangeability effective date (1 January 2025): VERIFIED (web, AASB 121 compiled text, standards.aasb.gov.au, which reproduces IAS 21). Indonesian effective date of the amendment: as stated in PHE Note 3b.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 229 (IAS 29), paras. 3, 8, 11–15, 27–28: VERIFIED (web, AASB 129, standards.aasb.gov.au).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"New numbers PSAK 221, PSAK 229, ISAK 122, PSAK 207, PSAK 212, PSAK 116: VERIFIED (web, IAI, \"Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia\", effective 1 January 2024). Old numbers (PSAK 10, 63, 46; ISAK 33) from the renumbering rule; content of ISAK 122 and PSAK 212.41: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"JISDOR Rp16,157 on 31 December 2024: VERIFIED (web, Bank Indonesia post on X, 31 Dec 2024; also implied by Note 48 to four decimals). Rp16,162, quoted elsewhere for that date, is BI's middle rate, not JISDOR. Rp15,439 at the end of 2023: VERIFIED (web, secondary: Kontan, 31 July 2024). Both match the rates implied by PHE Note 48.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"UU KUP art. 28(4) and (8): VERIFIED (web, secondary: pasal.id; Ortax). PMK 196/PMK.03/2007 as amended by PMK 24/PMK.011/2012, PMK 1/PMK.03/2015 and PMK 123/PMK.03/2019, taxpayer list including KKKS and dollar-functional taxpayers: VERIFIED (web, secondary: DDTC glossary citing PMK 123/2019 art. 3(1)); partial revocation by PMK 81/2024 from 1 January 2025: VERIFIED (web, JDIH Kemenkeu status page). Successor rules PMK 81/2024 art. 455(3) and PER-8/PJ/2025 art. 16(4)–(5): VERIFIED (web, secondary: Ortax, 15 July 2025). Which articles of PMK 196 were revoked: NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net open position cap of 20 percent of capital, PBI 5/13/PBI/2003 as last amended by PBI 17/5/PBI/2015: VERIFIED (web, bi.go.id; OJK). No revoking regulation found. Garuda Indonesia reporting in dollars since 2012: VERIFIED (web, secondary: Kontan; Indonesia Investments). The Indonesian corporate tax rate of 22 percent is as used across this series.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of foreign-currency transactions from choosing a functional currency to hyperinflation, worked through PHE's rupiah balances and set beside the industries that live with a currency mismatch","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["Every entity first determines its functional currency, the currency of the economic environment that mainly sets its prices and costs, from evidence the standard ranks. Any other currency is foreign. A Jakarta company can be a dollar company, and a Paris one can be too.","Foreign-currency transactions are recorded at the spot rate on the transaction date. At each reporting date, monetary items (cash, receivables, payables, provisions to be paid in cash) are retranslated at the closing rate and the difference goes to profit or loss. Non-monetary items carried at cost (inventory, fixed assets, prepayments) keep their historical rate.","An exchange loss therefore measures the net monetary position held in a foreign currency and how the rate moved while it was held. A dollar-functional company that holds more rupiah assets than rupiah liabilities loses when the rupiah weakens. Translation of a foreign operation into a presentation currency is a different step that goes to other comprehensive income, and hyperinflation (PSAK 229) adds a restatement before any translation."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = '5910e0a4-08c0-46f4-af2f-8d14c0133676' AND updated_at = '2026-10-12T09:47:08.318531+00:00'::timestamptz;
